{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 140 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "0ee5b31b52466390348ed975545412845fc4aec1da0231b48119fdeba0353f1a" :edb/db-before-t 139 :edb/db-after-t 140 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533452 50 #inst "2026-10-10T22:37:29.296Z" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186059409 1071 "What is the negation of the following compound statement?\n\n$\\qquad$ $6$ is composite and $5\\!\\not{\\mid} \\, 3$." 13194139533452 17592186045585 false] [17592186059409 1071 "What is the negation of the following compound statement? \n\n$\\qquad$ $6$ is composite and $5\\!\\not{|} \\, 3.$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186059409 1074 "De Morgan's law posits that the negation of a conjunction of two statements is equivalent to the disjunction of the negated individual statements. It can be stated symbolically as follows.\n\n$$\n\\neg ( A \\land B) = \\neg A \\lor \\neg B\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of our statement is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(6\\text{ is composite}\\text{ and }5\\,\\text{⧸}|\\,3) & ≡¬((6\\text{ is composite})∧(5\\,\\text{⧸}|\\,3)) \\\\  & ≡¬(6\\text{ is composite})∨¬(5\\,\\text{⧸}|\\,3) \\\\  & ≡(6\\text{ is not composite})∨(5\\,|\\,3) \\\\  & ≡6\\text{ is not composite}\\text{ or }5\\,|\\,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186059409 1074 "De Morgan's law posits that the negation of a conjunction of two statements is equivalent to the disjunction of the negated individual statements. It can be stated symbolically as follows.\n\n$$\n\\neg (A \\land B) = \\neg A \\lor \\neg B\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of our statement is\n\n$$\n¬(6is compositeand5⧸\\begin{vmatrix}3) & ≡¬((6is composite)∧(5⧸\\end{vmatrix}3)); ≡¬(6is composite)∨¬(5⧸\\begin{vmatrix}3) \\\\ ≡(6is not composite)∨(5 \\mid 3) \\\\ ≡6is not compositeor5\\end{vmatrix}3\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 false] [17592186059410 1074 "De Morgan's law for disjunctions states that the negation of a disjunction of two statements is equivalent to the conjunction of the negations of the individual statements. It can be stated symbolically as follows:\n\n$$\n\\neg ( A \\lor B) = \\neg A \\land \\neg B.\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of our statement is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(3\\text{ is not prime}\\text{ or }\\pi \\text{ is not rational}) & ≡¬((3\\text{ is not prime})∨(\\pi \\text{ is not rational})) \\\\  & ≡¬(3\\text{ is not prime})∧¬(\\pi \\text{ is not rational}) \\\\  & ≡(3\\text{ is prime})∧(\\pi \\text{ is rational}) \\\\  & ≡3\\text{ is prime}\\text{ and }\\pi \\text{ is rational}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186059410 1074 "De Morgan's law for disjunctions states that the negation of a disjunction of two statements is equivalent to the conjunction of the negations of the individual statements. It can be stated symbolically as follows:\n\n$$\n\\neg (A \\lor B) = \\neg A \\land \\neg B\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of our statement is\n$¬(3is not primeorπis not rational) \\mid ≡¬((3is not prime)∨(πis not rational)); ≡¬(3is not prime)∧¬(πis not rational); ≡(3is prime)∧(πis rational); ≡3is primeandπis rational$." 13194139533452 17592186045585 false] [17592186059411 1071 "Simplify the statement $\\neg ( (P \\lor \\neg Q) \\land (Q \\lor \\neg P) )$ using De Morgan's laws." 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186059411 1071 "Simplify the statement $\\neg ((P \\lor \\neg Q) \\land (Q \\lor \\neg P))$ using De Morgan's laws." 13194139533452 17592186045585 false] [17592186059411 1074 "De Morgan's laws state that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(P∧Q)≡¬P∨¬Q, \\\\ ¬(P∨Q)≡¬P∧¬Q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can simplify the given statement as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬((P∨¬Q)∧(Q∨¬P)) & ≡¬(P∨¬Q)∨¬(Q∨¬P) \\\\  & ≡(¬P∧¬(¬Q))∨(¬Q∧¬(¬P)) \\\\  & ≡(¬P∧Q)∨(¬Q∧P)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186059411 1074 "De Morgan's laws state that\n\n$$\n¬(P∧Q)≡¬P∨¬Q,; ¬(P∨Q)≡¬P∧¬Q\n$$\n\nSo, we can simplify the given statement as follows:\n\n$$\n¬((P∨¬Q)∧(Q∨¬P)) \\mid ≡¬(P∨¬Q)∨¬(Q∨¬P); ≡(¬P∧¬(¬Q))∨(¬Q∧¬(¬P)); ≡(¬P∧Q)∨(¬Q∧P)\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 false] [17592186059412 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- **If** $2$ is an even number, **then** $3$ is an odd number.\n\n- **If** $2$ is an even number, **then** $3$ is an even number.\n\n- **If** $2$ is an odd number, **then** $3$ is an odd number." 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186059412 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. **If** $2$ is an even number, **then** $3$ is an odd number.\n2. **If** $2$ is an even number, **then** $3$ is an even number.\n3. **If** $2$ is an odd number, **then** $3$ is an odd number." 13194139533452 17592186045585 false] [17592186059412 1074 "An implication $A \\Rightarrow B$ is true *unless* $A$ is true and $B$ is false. In other words, the implication is true unless the \"promise\" is broken.\n\n- Statement I is true. The antecedent \"$2$ is an even number\" is true, and the consequent \"$3$ is an odd number\" is true. So the promise is kept, and the statement is true.\n\n- Statement II is false. The antecedent \"$2$ is an even number\" is true, and the consequent \"$3$ is an even number\" is false. So the promise is broken, and the statement is false.\n\n- Statement III is true. The antecedent \"$2$ is an odd number\" is false, so the promise does not apply. Regardless of the consequent, the promise is *not* broken, and the statement is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186059412 1074 "An implication $A \\Rightarrow B$ is true *unless* $A$ is true and $B$ is false. In other words, the implication is true unless the \"promise\" is broken.\n\n- Statement I is true. The antecedent \"$2$ is an even number\" is true, and the consequent \"$3$ is an odd number\" is true. So the promise is kept, and the statement is true.\n- Statement II is false. The antecedent \"$2$ is an even number\" is true, and the consequent \"$3$ is an even number\" is false. So the promise is broken, and the statement is false.\n- Statement III is true. The antecedent \"$2$ is an odd number\" is false, so the promise does not apply. Regardless of the consequent, the promise is *not* broken, and the statement is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533452 17592186045585 false] [17592186059413 1071 "What are the missing values in the following truth table?\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\, \\neg A \\Rightarrow (A \\land B) \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{F}}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{F}}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{F}}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{F}}$ |" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186059413 1071 "What are the missing values in the following truth table?\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\, \\neg A \\Rightarrow (A \\land B) \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{F}}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{F}}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{F}}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{F}}$ |" 13194139533452 17592186045585 false] [17592186059413 1074 "First, we add columns for\n\n$$\n\\neg A\n$$\n\nand $A \\land B{:}$\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\, \\neg A \\,$ | $\\, A \\land B \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n\nNow, we add a column for\n\n$$\n\\neg A \\Rightarrow (A \\land B)\n$$\n\nThis statement is true unless\n\n$$\n\\neg A\n$$\n\nis true and $A \\land B$ is false.\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\, \\neg A \\,$ | $\\, A \\land B \\,$ | $\\, \\neg A \\Rightarrow (A \\land B) \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |" 13194139533452 17592186045585 false] [17592186059413 1074 "First, we add columns for $\\neg A$ and $A \\land B{:}$\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\, \\neg A \\,$ | $\\, A \\land B \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n\nNow, we add a column for $\\neg A \\Rightarrow (A \\land B).$ This statement is true unless $\\neg A$ is true and $A \\land B$ is false.\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\, \\neg A \\,$ | $\\, A \\land B \\,$ | $\\, \\neg A \\Rightarrow (A \\land B) \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186059414 1071 "If $A$ is true, then $A \\Rightarrow B$ is $\\text{______________}$.\n\nWhich of the following could fill in the blank space above to make a true statement?\n\n1. always true\n2. always false\n3. sometimes false" 13194139533452 17592186045585 false] [17592186059414 1071 "If $A$ is true, then $A \\Rightarrow B$ is $\\text{______________}.$\n\nWhich of the following could fill in the blank space above to make a true statement?\n\n- always true\n\n- always false\n\n- sometimes false" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186059414 1074 "The statement $A \\Rightarrow B$ is true *unless* $A$ is true and $B$ is false.\n\nLet's break this down into cases:\n\n- When $A = \\textrm T$ and $B = \\textrm T$, we have\n$(A⇒B) \\mid ≡(T⇒T); ≡T$.\n- When $A = \\textrm T$ and $B = \\textrm F$, we have\n$(A⇒B) \\mid ≡(T⇒F); ≡F$.\n\nTherefore, if $A$ is true, then $A \\Rightarrow B$ is $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{sometimes false}}$.\n\nThe complete truth table for $A\\Rightarrow B$ is shown below:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,A \\Rightarrow B\\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533452 17592186045585 false] [17592186059414 1074 "The statement $A \\Rightarrow B$ is true *unless* $A$ is true and $B$ is false. \n\nLet's break this down into cases:\n\n- When $A = \\textrm T$ and $B = \\textrm T,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A⇒B) & ≡(\\text{T}⇒\\text{T}) \\\\  & ≡\\text{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- When $A = \\textrm T$ and $B = \\textrm F,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A⇒B) & ≡(\\text{T}⇒\\text{F}) \\\\  & ≡\\text{F}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $A$ is true, then $A \\Rightarrow B$ is $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{sometimes false}}.$\n\nThe complete truth table for $A\\Rightarrow B$ is