7592186045585 true] [17592186064327 1038 #edb/ref 17592186376034 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186064636 1068 #edb/ref 17592186061843 13194139533452 17592186045585 false] [17592186064636 1068 #edb/ref 17592186376975 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186064636 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186064636 1071 "Cars are entering a traffic jam at a rate of $7t$ cars per minute. If at time $t = 0$ there are $60$ cars in the traffic jam, write an expression for $A(t)$, the total number of cars caught up in the traffic jam at time $t$, where $t$ is the time measured in minutes.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533452 17592186045585 false] [17592186064636 1071 "Cars are entering a traffic jam at a rate of $7t$ cars per minute. If at time $t=0$ there are $60$ cars in the traffic jam, write an expression for $A(t),$ the total number of cars caught up in the traffic jam at time $t,$ where $t$ is the time measured in minutes." 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186064636 1074 "Let $f(t)=7t$ be the rate at which the cars enter the traffic jam, and let $A(T)$ be the total number of cars in the traffic jam after $T$ minutes.\n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nA(T)=A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(T) & =A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =60+{\\int }_{0}^{T}7t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={60+(\\frac{7{t}^{2}}{2})|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =60+\\frac{7{T}^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable $t.$ We have\n\n$$\nA(t)=60+\\frac{7{t}^{2}}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186068926 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375992 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186068927 1038 #edb/ref 17592186377571 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186068927 1038 #edb/ref 17592186377600 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186069268 1068 #edb/ref 17592186066748 13194139533452 17592186045585 false] [17592186069268 1068 #edb/ref 17592186377327 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186069268 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186069268 1071 "$\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186069268 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int\\frac{\\ln^{2} x}{x^{3}}dx$?" 13194139533452 17592186045585 false] [17592186069268 1074 "We need to use integration by parts.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{2}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({ln}^{2}⁡(x))(-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}})-\\int (-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}})(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡(x)}{2{x}^{2}}+\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{{x}^{3}}\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x))(-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}})-\\int (-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}})(\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{2{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{2{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡(x)}{2{x}^{2}}+(-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{2{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}+C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡(x)}{2{x}^{2}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{2{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}(2{ln}^{2}⁡(x)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186078315 1071 "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^5 f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\\,?$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3, & x<0 \\\\ 2x+3, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 false] [17592186078315 1071 "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^5 f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\\,?$\n![](images/25/256fe45a207e24c746e9b00eb92fa6d32404d240df3916d1536968592f117700.png)" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186078462 1038 #edb/ref 17592186376420 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186078588 1038 #edb/ref 17592186376020 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186078603 1038 #edb/ref 17592186377357 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186078603 1038 #edb/ref 17592186377360 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186078603 1038 #edb/ref 17592186377363 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186078603 1038 #edb/ref 17592186377366 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186078604 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375796 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186078604 1038 #edb/ref 17592186377076 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186078604 1038 #edb/ref 17592186377282 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186078605 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375803 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186078605 1038 #edb/ref 17592186377574 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186083350 1038 #edb/ref 17592186377541 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186083843 1068 #edb/ref 17592186081959 13194139533452 17592186045585 false] [17592186083843 1068 #edb/ref 17592186377621 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186083843 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186083843 1071 "${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186083843 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{∞}\\frac{1}{2x + 1}dx$?" 13194139533452 17592186045585 false] [17592186083843 1074 "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2a+1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1|) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2a+1|) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\infty ) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent." 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186087806 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375672 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186087806 1038 #edb/ref 17592186377049 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186087806 1038 #edb/ref 17592186377054 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186087806 1038 #edb/ref 17592186377059 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186087806 1038 #edb/ref 17592186377906 13194139533452 17592186045585