f two line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186101543 1074 "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-3,0]$  and the line segment for $x\\in [0,4].$ Therefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,0].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4+1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,4].$ The corresponding region is rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =lw \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}+4 \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186105742 1038 #edb/ref 17592186377082 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186105743 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375887 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186105743 1038 #edb/ref 17592186377085 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186105867 1038 #edb/ref 17592186376081 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186106133 1068 #edb/ref 17592186102644 13194139533452 17592186045585 false] [17592186106133 1068 #edb/ref 17592186376704 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186106133 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186106133 1071 "Let $p = ⟨2,-1, 0⟩$, $q = ⟨3,-3, 2⟩$ and $r = ⟨1,-4, 1⟩$. Find $r \\cdot (q \\cdot p)$." 13194139533452 17592186045585 false] [17592186106133 1071 "Let $p=⟨2,-1,0⟩$, $q=⟨3,-3,2⟩$ and $r=⟨1,-4,1⟩.$ Find $r\\cdot (q\\times p).$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186106133 1074 "Remember that the scalar triple product $r\\cdot (q\\times p)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $r,$ $q,$ and $p.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot (q\\times p) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}|-(-4)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|+1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =((-3)\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot 2)+4(3\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 2)+(3\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot (-3)) \\\\  & =2-16+3 \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186115241 1038 #edb/ref 17592186377674 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186115242 1038 #edb/ref 17592186377581 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186119508 1074 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ for any vector $\\mathbf{v}$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $\\Pi$ contains vectors of the form \n\n![](images/d6/d64a36f48b3ad9b9c4fb1426260daa832254ec37abea4b1b33fafeb793aa1798.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The plane does not pass through the origin since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4y-3z & =2 \\\\ (0)+4(0)-3(0) & =2 \\\\ 0 & =2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider ![](images/2e/2e5c2dab37c97d3f13e1ce3c981a31d51d46a64daa6f21e2102747afbc84d015.png) and ![](images/19/19be68b7d6cf1b05832601c75bb456ec66a9f15f56188c89bf9ba98ad83d937d.png) Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2+1 \\\\ 0+1 \\\\ 0+1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ does not belong to $\\Pi$ since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4y-3z & =2 \\\\ (3)+4(1)-3(1) & =2 \\\\ 4 & =2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\mathbf{0} \\not\\in \\Pi,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $\\Pi$ is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186119508 1074 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ for any vector $\\mathbf{v}$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $\\Pi$ contains vectors of the form \n\n![](images/ff/ff70a23af671918e56523ccd14c7c52bcafe502b3d5656f4e7a3f47606eda810.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The plane does not pass through the origin since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4y-3z & =2 \\\\ (0)+4(0)-3(0) & =2 \\\\ 0 & =2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider ![](images/d0/d08091d57940e8ef91dc371a628eae1c1dc7395cf2d284f48915593116a59510.png) and ![](images/31/3117536c9d0c6d9e228d909405e8542abdc50dfda5cef00e92719086d795855f.png) Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2+1 \\\\ 0+1 \\\\ 0+1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ does not belong to $\\Pi$ since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4y-3z & =2 \\\\ (3)+4(1)-3(1) & =2 \\\\ 4 & =2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\mathbf{0} \\not\\in \\Pi,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $\\Pi$ is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" 13194139533452 17592186045585 false] [17592186119562 1071 "![](images/19/1990d7cb1625ca97f10debf834883f6e434473f154cf9683db60ee7c4a69c83e.png)\n\nFind a parametric equation of the osculating plane for the curve  $\\mathbf r(t)$ at the point where $t=1,$ given that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\left\\langle -2t , \\: t^2 , \\: \\dfrac{1}{3}t^3 \\right\\rangle, \\qquad\n\\mathbf T(1) = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\left\\langle -2 , 2 , 1 \\right\\rangle, \\qquad\n\\mathbf N(1) = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\left\\langle 2 , 1 , 2\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186119562 1071 "![](images/19/1990d7cb1625ca97f10debf834883f6e434473f154cf9683db60ee7c4a69c83e.png)\n\nFind a parametric equation of the osculating plane for the curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ at the point where $t=1$, given that\n\n$\\mathbf r(t) = \\left\\langle -2t, \\: t^2, \\: \\dfrac{1}{3}t^3 \\right\\rangle, \\qquad \\mathbf T(1) = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\left\\langle -2, 2, 1 \\right\\rangle, \\qquad \\mathbf N(1) = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\left\\langle 2, 1, 2\\right\\rangle$.\n\nNote that $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." 13194139533452 17592186045585 false] [17592186119562 1074 "The osculating plane passes through the point $P$, which is at the tip of the vector $\\mathbf r(1)$. The vectors $\\mathbf T(1)$ and $\\mathbf N(1)$ are parallel to this plane and are not collinear, so a parametric equation for the plane can be written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf R(s,u) = \\mathbf r(1) + s \\, \\mathbf T(1) + u \\, \\mathbf N(1), \\qquad s,u \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r(1)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(1) &= ⟨ - 2(1), (1)^{2}, \\frac{1}{3}(1)^{3}⟩ = ⟨ - 2, 1, \\frac{1}{3}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a parametric equation for the osculating plane at the point\n\n$$\n\\left(-2,1,\\dfrac 1 3 \\right)\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR(s, u) &= r(1) + sT(1) + uN(1) \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 2, 1, \\frac{1}{3}⟩ + s \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨ - 2, 2, 1⟩ + u \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨2, 1, 2⟩ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}⟨ - 6 - 2s + 2u, 3 + 2s + u, 1 + s + 2u⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 false] [17592186119562 1074 "The osculating plane passes through the point $P,$ which is at the tip of the vector $\\mathbf r(1).$ The vectors $\\mathbf T(1)$ and $\\mathbf N(1)$ are parallel to this plane and are not collinear, so a parametric equation for the plane can be written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf R(s,u) = \\mathbf r(1) + s \\, \\mathbf T(1) + u \\, \\mathbf N(1), \\qquad s,u \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing $\\mathbf r(1),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨-2(1),\\,(1)^{2},\\,\\frac{1}{3}(1)^{3}⟩=⟨-2,1,\\frac{1}{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a parametric equation for the osculating plane at the point $\\left(-2,1,\\dfrac 1 3 \\right)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s,u) & =r(1)+s\\,T(1)+u\\,N(1) \\\\  & =⟨-2,1,\\frac{1}{3}⟩+s\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨-2,2,1⟩+u\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨2,1,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-6-2s+2u,\\,3+2s+u,\\,1+s+2u⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186119563 1071 "Find a Cartesian equation of the osculating plane for the curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ at the point where $t=0$, given that\n\n$r(t)|= \\cos ti + \\cos tj + \\sqrt{2}\\sin tk; T(t)|= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(-\\sin ti - \\sin tj + \\sqrt{2}\\cos tk); N(t)|=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\cos ti + \\cos tj + \\sqrt{2}\\sin tk)$.\n\nNote that $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." 13194139533452 17592186045585 false] [17592186119563 1071 "Find a Cartesian equation of the osculating plane for the curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ at the point where $t=0,$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k \\\\ T(t) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k) \\\\ N(t) & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡tk).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186119563 1074 "To calculate the Cartesian equation of the osculating plane, we need to compute the position vector $\\mathbf p$ and a vector $\\mathbf n$ that is perpendicular to the osculating plane.\n\nThe position vector of the point where $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p} = \\mathbf{r}(0) = \\cos{0}\\,\\mathbf i+\\cos{0}\\,\\mathbf j+\\sqrt{2}\\sin{0}\\, \\mathbf k = \\langle 1, 1, 0 \\rangle\n$$\n\nTo compute the perpendicular vector, we first need to compute the unit tangent $\\mathbf T(0)$ and the principal normal $\\mathbf N(0)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(0) &= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(-\\sin 0i - \\sin 0j + \\sqrt{2}\\cos 0k) = k \\\\\nN(0) &= -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\cos 0i + \\cos 0j + \\sqrt{2}\\sin 0k) =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i + j)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take the cross product of these two vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= k \\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i + j)) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\cdot i\\begin{vmatrix}j & k \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}1 \\\\\n&= 1 \\mid 1\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\\\ =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}[0\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}0i - 0\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}0j + 0 \\mid 0 \\\\\n&= 1\\end{vmatrix}1k] \\\\\n&=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(-i + j) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i - j)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are interested only in the direction of the vector above, we can multiply it by a constant to yield a simpler vector that points in the same direction:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n} = \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt 2}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\left(\\mathbf i-\\mathbf j\\right) =\\mathbf i-\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nFinally, let $\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ denote the position vector of a general point in the osculating plane. Since the plane is perpendicular to $\\mathbf{n}$ and passes through the tip of the position vector $\\mathbf{p}$, its Cartesian equation can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(⟨x, y, z⟩ - p) \\cdot n &= 0 \\\\\n⟨x, y, z⟩ \\cdot n - p \\cdot n &= 0 \\\\\n⟨x, y, z⟩ \\cdot n &= p \\cdot n \\\\\n⟨x, y, z⟩ \\cdot ⟨1,-1, 0⟩ &= ⟨1, 1, 0⟩ \\cdot ⟨1,-1, 0⟩ \\\\\n1 \\cdot x + (-1) \\cdot y + 0 \\cdot z &= 0 \\\\\nx - y &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/ad/ad22c676123844c2428febb34e0218cfcb05917154e8cff994f35fa4fbf68944.png)\n\n**Note:** Because our curve is a plane curve, it has the same osculating plane at each point. As shown above, the osculating plane coincides with the plane of the curve.\n\nThis result holds in general: any plane curve has the same osculating plane at each of its points (wherever the osculating plane is defined)." 13194139533452 17592186045585 false] [17592186119563 1074 "To calculate the Cartesian equation of the osculating plane, we need to compute the position vector $\\mathbf p$ and a vector $\\mathbf n$ that is perpendicular to the osculating plane. \n\nThe position vector of the point where $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p} = \\mathbf{r}(0) = \\cos{0}\\,\\mathbf i+\\cos{0}\\,\\mathbf j+\\sqrt{2}\\sin{0}\\, \\mathbf k = \\langle 1, 1, 0 \\rangle.\n$$\n\nTo compute the perpendicular vector, we first need to compute the unit tangent $\\mathbf T(0)$ and the principal normal $\\mathbf N(0)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0\\,j+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0\\,k)=k \\\\ N(0) & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡0\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0\\,j+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0k)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take the cross product of these two vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =k\\times (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(-i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are interested only in the direction of the vector above, we can multiply it by a constant to yield a simpler vector that points in the same direction:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n} = \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt 2}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\left(\\mathbf i-\\mathbf j\\right) =\\mathbf i-\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nFinally, let $\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ denote the position vector of a general point in the osculating plane. Since the plane is perpendicular to $\\mathbf{n}$ and passes through the tip of the position vector $\\mathbf{p},$ its Cartesian equation can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(⟨x,y,z⟩-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-1,0⟩ & =⟨1,1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-1,0⟩ \\\\ 1\\cdot x+(-1)\\cdot y+0\\cdot z & =0 \\\\ x-y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/ad/ad22c676123844c2428febb34e0218cfcb05917154e8cff994f35fa4fbf68944.png)\n\n**Note:** Because our curve is a plane curve, it has the same osculating plane at each point. As shown above, the osculating plane coincides with the plane of the curve. \n\nThis result holds in general: any plane curve has the same osculatin