585 false] [17592186166680 1071 "The velocity $\\mathbf v$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by $\\mathbf v(t)= \\left\\langle -4, \\, \\cos{(2t)},\\, \\sin{(2t)} \\right\\rangle.$ Find the normal component of the acceleration of $P$ at time $t.$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186166680 1074 "The normal component of acceleration is given by\n\n$$\na_N = \\dfrac{\\mid \\mid \\mathbf v \\times \\mathbf a \\mid \\mid}{\\mid \\mid \\mathbf v \\mid \\mid}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(-4), \\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos (2t)), \\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin (2t))⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨0,-2\\sin (2t), 2\\cos (2t)⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $\\mathbf v \\times \\mathbf a{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv \\cdot a &= ⟨ - 4, \\cos (2t), \\sin (2t)⟩ \\cdot ⟨0,-2\\sin (2t), 2\\cos (2t)⟩ \\\\\n&= i\\begin{vmatrix}j & k \\\\ -4 & \\cos (2t)\\end{vmatrix}\\sin (2t) \\\\\n&= 0\\begin{vmatrix}-2\\sin (2t) & 2\\cos (2t) \\\\ = \\cos (2t) & \\sin (2t) \\\\ -2\\sin (2t) & 2\\cos (2t)i - - 4\\end{vmatrix}\\sin (2t) \\\\\n&= 0 \\mid 2\\cos (2t)j + - 4\\begin{vmatrix}\\cos (2t) \\\\ 0\\end{vmatrix}- 2\\sin (2t)k \\\\\n&= ⟨2\\cos^{2} (2t) + 2\\sin^{2} (2t), 8\\cos (2t), 8\\sin (2t)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨2, 8\\cos (2t), 8\\sin (2t)⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $\\mid \\mid \\mathbf v \\mid \\mid$ and $\\mid \\mid \\mathbf v \\times \\mathbf a \\mid \\mid$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\mid v \\mid \\mid &= \\sqrt{(-4)^{2} + (\\cos (2t))^{2} + (\\sin (2t))^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{16 + \\cos^{2} (2t) + \\sin^{2} (2t)} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{16 + 1} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{17}, \\\\\n\\mid \\mid v \\cdot a \\mid \\mid &= \\sqrt{(2)^{2} + (8\\cos (2t))^{2} + (8\\sin (2t))^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4 + 64\\cos^{2} (2t) + 64\\sin^{2} (2t)} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4 + 64(\\cos^{2} (2t) + \\sin^{2} (2t))} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4 + 64} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{68} \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{17}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the normal component of the acceleration, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{N} &= \\frac{\\mid \\mid v \\cdot a \\mid \\mid}{\\mid \\mid v \\mid \\mid} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2\\sqrt{17}}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 false] [17592186166680 1074 "The normal component of acceleration is given by\n\n$$\na_N = \\dfrac{||\\mathbf v \\times \\mathbf a||}{||\\mathbf v||}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-4),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $\\mathbf v \\times \\mathbf a{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\times a & =⟨-4,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -4 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 0 & -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}-4 & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 0 & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}-4 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 0 & -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =⟨2{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+2{sin}^{2}⁡(2t),8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $||\\mathbf v||$ and $||\\mathbf v \\times \\mathbf a||\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||v|| & =\\sqrt{{(-4)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}, \\\\ ||v\\times a|| & =\\sqrt{{(2)}^{2}+{(8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))}^{2}+{(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+64{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+64{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+64({cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2t))} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+64} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{68} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the normal component of the acceleration, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{N} & =\\frac{||v\\times a||}{||v||} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{17}}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186166681 1071 "The velocity $\\mathbf v$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by $\\mathbf v(t) =  \\langle 1, \\, \\sqrt{2} t,\\, -t^2 \\rangle.$ Find the tangential component of acceleration at time $t.$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186166681 1071 "The velocity $\\mathbf v$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by $\\mathbf v(t) = \\langle 1, \\, \\sqrt{2} t,\\, -t^2 \\rangle$. Find the tangential component of acceleration at time $t$." 13194139533452 17592186045585 false] [17592186166681 1074 "The tangential component of acceleration is given by\n\n$$\na_T = \\dfrac{\\mathbf v \\cdot \\mathbf a}{\\mid \\mid \\mathbf v \\mid \\mid}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(1), \\frac{d}{dt}(\\sqrt{2}t), \\frac{d}{dt}(-t^{2})⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨0, \\sqrt{2},-2t⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $\\mathbf v \\cdot \\mathbf a$ and $\\mid \\mid \\mathbf v \\mid \\mid$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv \\cdot a &= ⟨1, \\sqrt{2}t,-t^{2}⟩ \\cdot ⟨0, \\sqrt{2},-2t⟩ \\\\\n&= 2t + 2t^{3} \\\\\n&= 2t(t^{2} + 1), \\\\\n\\mid \\mid v \\mid \\mid &= \\sqrt{(1)^{2} + (\\sqrt{2}t)^{2} + (-t^{2})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{t^{4} + 2t^{2} + 1} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(t^{2} + 1)^{2}} \\\\\n&= t^{2} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate tangentia