 initial value problem is\n\n$$\nx(t)=1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+5\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186167286 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164899 13194139533452 17592186045585 false] [17592186167286 1068 #edb/ref 17592186377333 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186167286 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186167286 1071 "$\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186167286 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int\\sin (5x)\\cos (2x)dx$?" 13194139533452 17592186045585 false] [17592186167286 1074 "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for sine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+2x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-2x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186167334 1068 #edb/ref 17592186165183 13194139533452 17592186045585 false] [17592186167334 1068 #edb/ref 17592186378038 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186167334 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186167334 1071 "Let $f(x, y, z) = x^{2} + 2xy - 3z^{2}$, where $x = st$, $y = s + 3t$, and $z = 2s - t$. Given that\n$\\frac{∂f}{∂t} = 2(s^{2}t + s^{2} + c_{1}st + c_{2}s + c_{3}t)$,\n\nfind the value of $c_{1} + c_{2} + c_{3}$." 13194139533452 17592186045585 false] [17592186167334 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+2xy-3{z}^{2},$ where $x=st,$ $y=s+3t,$ and $z=2s-t.$ Given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=2({s}^{2}t+{s}^{2}+{c}_{1}st+{c}_{2}s+{c}_{3}t),\n$$\n\nfind the value of ${c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+{c}_{3}.$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186167334 1074 "![](images/b5/b593a1b60fe8c1aef2c5c229ff426db350e6296b9ecb767494283f0fa3342072.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x+2y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-6z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2(st)+2(s+3t)=2st+2s+6t \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2st \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =-6(2s-t)=6t-12s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =s \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =3 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂t} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =(2st+2s+6t)\\cdot s+(2st)\\cdot 3+(6t-12s)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =2{s}^{2}t+2{s}^{2}+6st+6st-6t+12s \\\\  & =2{s}^{2}t+2{s}^{2}+12st+12s-6t \\\\  & =2({s}^{2}t+{s}^{2}+6st+6s-3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${c}_{1}={c}_{2}=6,{c}_{3}=-3,$ and therefore\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+{c}_{3}=6+6-3=9.\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186167495 1068 #edb/ref 17592186166148 13194139533452 17592186045585 false] [17592186167495 1068 #edb/ref 17592186378078 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186167495 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186167495 1071 "The velocity $v$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by $v(t) = ⟨e^{t},-2e^{-2t}, 1⟩$. Find the tangential component of acceleration at time $t$." 13194139533452 17592186045585 false] [17592186167495 1071 "The velocity $v$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by $v(t)=⟨{e}^{t},\\,-2{e}^{-2t},\\,1⟩.$ Find the tangential component of acceleration at time $t.$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186167495 1074 "The tangential component of acceleration is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{T}=\\frac{v\\cdot a}{||v||}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2{e}^{-2t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,4{e}^{-2t},\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $v\\cdot a$ and $||v||:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot a & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,-2{e}^{-2t},\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨{e}^{t},\\,4{e}^{-2t},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}-8{e}^{-4t}, \\\\ ||v|| & =\\sqrt{{({e}^{t})}^{2}+{(2{e}^{-2t})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+4{e}^{-4t}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate tangential component of the acceleration, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{T} & =\\frac{v\\cdot a}{||v||} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2t}-8{e}^{-4t}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+4{e}^{-4t}+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186167496 1068 #edb/ref 17592186166154 13194139533452 17592186045585 false] [17592186167496 1068 #edb/ref 17592186378084 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186167496 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186167496 1071 "The position $r$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by $r(t) = ⟨2t, t^{3}, t^{2}⟩$. Find the tangential component of the acceleration of $P$ at time $t$." 13194139533452 17592186045585 false] [17592186167496 1071 "The position $r$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by $r(t)=⟨2t,\\,{t}^{3},\\,{t}^{2}⟩.$ Find the tangential component of the acceleration of $P$ at time $t.$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186167496 1074 "The tangential component of acceleration is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{T}=\\frac{v\\cdot a}{||v||}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,3{t}^{2},\\,2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,6t,\\,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute $v\\cdot a$ and $||v||:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot a & =⟨2,\\,3{t}^{2},\\,2t⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,6t,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =18{t}^{3}+4t \\\\ ||v|| & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(3{t}^{2})^{2}+(2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+9{t}^{4}+4{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the tangential component of the acceleration, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{T} & =\\frac{v\\cdot a}{||v||} \\\\  & =\\frac{18{t}^{3}+4t}{\\sqrt{4+9{t}^{4}+4{t}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186167497 1068 #edb/ref 17592186166160 13194139533452 17592186045585 false] [17592186167497 1068 #edb/ref 17592186378090 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186167497 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186167497 1071 "The velocity $v$ of a particle $P$ at time $t > 0$ is given by $v(t) = ⟨2t, t^{2}, 0⟩$. Find the normal component of the acceleration of $P$ at time $t$." 13194139533452 17592186045585 false] [17592186167497 1071 "The velocity $v$ of a particle $P$ at time $t>0$ is given by $v(t)=⟨2t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,0⟩.$ Find the normal component of the acceleration of $P$ at time $t.$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186167497 1074 "The normal component of acceleration is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{N}=\\frac{||v\\times a||}{||v||}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2t,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $v\\times a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\times a & =⟨2t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,0⟩\\times ⟨2,\\,2t,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2t & {t}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2t & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 2t & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}2t & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}2t & {t}^{2} \\\\ 2 & 2t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =⟨0,0,4{t}^{2}-2{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,0,2{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $||v||$ and $||v\\times a||:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||v|| & =\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+({t}^{2})^{2}+(0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{2}(4+{t}^{2})} \\\\  & =t\\sqrt{4+{t}^{2}} \\\\ ||v\\times a|| & =\\sqrt{(0)^{1}+(0)^{2}+(2{t}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the normal component of the acceleration, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{N} & =\\frac{||v\\times a||}{||v||} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{t}^{2}}{t\\sqrt{4+{t}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{\\sqrt{4+{t}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186171930 1038 #edb/ref 17592186376497 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186171952 1038 #edb/ref 17592186376334 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186171957 1038 #edb/ref 17592186376720 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186172094 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375810 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186175952 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375740 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186175999 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375726 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186175999 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375733 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186176238 1068 #edb/ref 17592186173656 13194139533452 17592186045585 false] [17592186176238 1068 #edb/ref 17592186375720 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186176238 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186176238 1071 "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=\\lambda (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -5)^{7},$ the nullity of $(A-5I)^{2}$ equals $1,$ and the nullity of $(A-5I)^{3}$ equals $2.$ If a basis of ${R}^{9}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5?$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186176238 1071 "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(λ) = λ(λ - 1)(λ - 5)^{7}$, the nullity of $(A - 5I)^{2}$ equals $1$, and the nullity of $(A - 5I)^{3}$ equals $2$. If a basis of $R^{9}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ = 5$?" 13194139533452 17592186045585 false] [17592186176238 1074 "For the given matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =5,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(A-5I)^{3}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-5I)^{2}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(A-5I)^{2}$ has dimension $1.$ This means that the solutions to $(A-5I)^{2}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $1.$\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(A-5I)^{3}$ has dimension $2.$ This means that the solutions to $(A-5I)^{3}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $2.$\n\nNow, since $\\text{Null}(A-5I)^{2}$ is a subspace of dimension $1$ in $\\text{Null}(A-5I)^{3}$, which is a subspace of dimension $2,$ our basis can contain only $1$ vector that lies in $\\text{Null}(A-5I)^{3}$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(A-5I)^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-5I)^{3}-\\text{nullity}(A-5I)^{2}=2-1=1\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5.$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186179785 1038 #edb/ref 17592186376137 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186184166 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375922 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186184166 1038 #edb/ref 17592186377741 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186184177 1038 #edb/ref 17592186377762 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186184178 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375950 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186184178 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375978 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186184178 1038 #edb/ref 17592186377769 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186198294 1038 #edb/ref 17592186376177 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186198307 1038 #edb/ref 17592186376211 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186198341 1038 #edb/ref 17592186376596 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186198344 1038 #edb/ref 17592186376897 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186198351 1038 #edb/ref 17592186376558 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [175921861984