k.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}\n$$\n\nand $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{min}$. Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which coagulant enters the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.05\\,\\textrm{kg/L}$ of the coagulant enters the tank at a rate of $30\\,\\textrm{L/min}$. So, the rate at which the *coagulant* enters the tank is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ_{in}^{′} &= (0.05\\frac{kg}{L}) \\cdot (30\\frac{L}{min}) \\\\\n&= (0.05\\frac{kg}{L}) \\cdot (30\\frac{L}{min}) \\\\\n&= 1.5\\frac{kg}{min}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which coagulant exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which coagulant leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t$.\n\nThe tank initially contains $700\\,\\textrm{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $30\\,\\textrm{L/min}$ and leaves at $20\\,\\textrm{L/min}$.\nAs a result, the volume of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is\n\n$$\n700 + 30t - 20t = (700+10t)\\,\\textrm{L}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of coagulant in the tank, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{700+10t}\\,\\textrm{kg/L}\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $20\\,\\textrm{L/min}$, *coagulant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ_{out}^{′} &= (20\\frac{L}{min}) \\cdot (\\frac{Q}{700 + 10t}\\frac{kg}{L}) \\\\\n&= (20\\frac{L}{min}) \\cdot (\\frac{Q}{700 + 10t}\\frac{kg}{L}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{20Q}{700 + 10t}\\frac{kg}{min} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2Q}{70 + t}\\frac{kg}{min}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dQ}{dt} &= Q_{in}^{′} - Q_{out}^{′} \\\\\n&= 1.5 - \\fr