7" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375630 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375630 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375630 1072 #uuid "8b4d7dbc-be4c-5d12-8115-af652723cf38" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375630 1074 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡t=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$ by ${cos}^{2}⁡t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{{cos}^{2}⁡t}+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t}{{cos}^{2}⁡t} & =\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\ {(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})}^{2}+1 & ={(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})}^{2} \\\\ {tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2}$ and $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t=y$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}+1={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${y}^{2}={(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}+1.$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375631 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375632 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375631 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375633 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375631 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375634 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375631 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375635 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375631 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375636 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375631 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375631 1077 "selection" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375631 1079 #edb/ref 17592186375634 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375631 1080 #uuid "b5fabfe3-808f-535a-8196-05aa4185848e" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375632 1081 "{y}^{2}={(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}-1" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375632 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375632 1084 #uuid "b587af18-df81-5db4-88ae-e75eb49804be" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375633 1081 "{y}^{2}={(\\frac{2}{x})}^{2}+1" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375633 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375633 1084 #uuid "42dbc938-1217-5910-be74-c7acaaa09ee3" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375634 1081 "{y}^{2}={(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}+1" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375634 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375634 1084 #uuid "d880a9fe-7ede-58b5-95dc-503280226675" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375635 1081 "{y}^{2}={x}^{2}+2" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375635 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375635 1084 #uuid "c09ce9e9-e44a-5f6f-a0ba-e4f831714695" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375636 1081 "{y}^{2}=1-{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375636 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375636 1084 #uuid "394ebbf2-c2d3-501a-9d24-4f85814641c7" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375637 1068 #edb/ref 17592186375638 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375637 1069 "q-103482" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375637 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375637 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- **If** $-5>-2,$ **then** $-2<-1.$\n- **If** $-5<-2,$ **then** $-2<-1.$\n- **If** $-5>-2,$ **then** $-2>-1.$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375637 1072 #uuid "d0b7a4cb-746e-55f4-a074-7c8a26769a8e" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375637 1074 "An implication $A⇒B$ is true *unless* $A$ is true and $B$ is false. In other words, the implication is true unless the \"promise\" is broken.\n\n- Statement I is true. The antecedent $-5>-2$ is false, so the promise does not apply. Regardless of the consequent, the promise is *not* broken, and the statement is true.\n\n- Statement II is true. The antecedent $-5<-2$ is true, and the consequent $-2<-1$ is true. So the promise is kept, and the statement is true.\n\n- Statement III is true. Again, the antecedent $-5>-2$ is false, so the promise does not apply. Regardless of the consequent, the promise is *not* broken, and the statement is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375638 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375639 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375638 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375640 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375638 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375641 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375638 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375642 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375638 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375643 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375638 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375638 1077 "selection" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375638 1079 #edb/ref 17592186375639 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375638 1080 #uuid "377f97f1-5b53-5212-9e2e-8673b4f6d3ac" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375639 1081 "I, II, and III" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375639 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375639 1084 #uuid "6efdda93-e488-52a9-b295-ea564a97fa74" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375640 1081 "II and III only" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375640 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375640 1084 #uuid "91700a18-c990-5969-9161-60b52a4708a8" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375641 1081 "I and III only" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375641 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375641 1084 #uuid "45bc3fe5-3764-5087-a16f-9bae2c1e651d" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375642 1081 "III only" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375642 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375642 1084 #uuid "d1fb98c7-efc1-544d-83fe-b3a5be20f7a6" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375643 1081 "II only" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375643 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375643 1084 #uuid "36d6a570-45e1-5e04-b7d8-6e8debd788f0" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375644 1068 #edb/ref 17592186375645 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375644 1069 "q-105331" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375644 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375644 1071 "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}<36},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(0,-3)$ belongs to $A$\n- The point $P(0,-3)$ is a boundary point of $A$\n- The point $Q(0,2)$ is an interior point of $A$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375644 1072 #uuid "5661c06a-c66c-5923-befb-278bbbf8f25e" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375644 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie inside the ellipse $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/f3/f3b1df5f948603edcc1095da9215145cba21c7aebbdfdd6ab5cacecb5810efc7.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(0,-3)$ and $Q(0,2)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/7b/7bc1b41e3ca8aba5c1a5658ec421f7358041c57dea7c1ef45fe265c62664f097.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of $P(0,-3)$ do not satisfy the inequality $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}<36.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(0,-3)$ satisfy the equation $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $Q$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $Q$ that is fully contained inside the set $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only'." 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375645 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375646 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375645 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375647 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375645 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375648 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375645 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375649 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375645 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375650 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375645 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375645 1077 "selection" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375645 1079 #edb/ref 17592186375646 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375645 1080 #uuid "2c13b258-02bc-571c-be22-4efddeb6b233" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375646 1081 "II and III only" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375646 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375646 1084 #uuid "06271d57-0934-56eb-96a3-ed1870802e48" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375647 1081 "I and III only" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375647 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375647 1084 #uuid "1d04450e-1ff2-5843-b13c-4c5390fa92e0" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375648 1081 "III only" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375648 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375648 1084 #uuid "878c1638-baba-553a-a018-9f2643bd35af" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375649 1081 "I and II only" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375649 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375649 1084 #uuid "cfae8cea-0e54-5226-8425-c7d769e17697" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375650 1081 "II only" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375650 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375650 1084 #uuid "8dfdd82f-d34b-595b-ba15-afb3566e61f9" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375651 1068 #edb/ref 17592186375652 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375651 1069 "q-105336" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375651 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375651 1071 "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}\\ge 36},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=36}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(3,2)$ belongs to $A$\n- The point $P(3,2)$ is a boundary point of $A$\n- The point $Q(3,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375651 1072 #uuid "ab42b0ba-ecca-5e90-984d-305864760335" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375651 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or outside the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=36.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/67/67d1f23f424561c69973fe5f08ad3ee3a03346f555db8f00fb71406c02a9cdf9.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(3,2)$ and $Q(3,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/0b/0b3e72b3a65241c4e03716a9c0b534d687c305a92ceef0b92d5c6df8c1b82953.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(3,2)$ satisfy the inequality $4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}\\ge 36.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The coordinates of $P(3,2)$ do not satisfy the equation $4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=36.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie on the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Every open neighborhood of $Q$ contains points that belong to $A$ and points that do not belong to $A.$ The point $Q$ is a boundary point for the given set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only'." 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375652 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375653 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375652 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375654 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375652 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375655 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375652 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375656 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375652 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375657 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375652 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375652 1077 "selection" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375652 1079 #edb/ref 17592186375655 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375652 1080 #uuid "e6e607c8-1e9d-51ff-8b16-34e90c6a063d" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375653 1081 "II and III only" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375653 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375653 1084 #uuid "aba4ba05-5975-5600-91df-d4ebfc647f67" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375654 1081 "I and III only" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375654 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375654 1084 #uuid "e445dc1c-7bdc-584c-8de4-0830a84c0097" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375655 1081 "I only" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375655 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375655 1084 #uuid "95f17243-2279-538f-881c-66982c2023e4" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375656 1081 "III only" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375656 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375656 1084 #uuid "0bb08f16-51f2-54c1-8fa9-6bd2c24d500f" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375657 1081 "II only" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375657 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375657 1084 #uuid "a9f87a35-83a7-5600-83d3-0210752e8c54" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375658 1068 #edb/ref 17592186375659 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375658 1069 "q-105510" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375658 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375658 1071 "Let $V={R}_{2}[t],$ the vector space of polynomials of degree at most $2$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $p(t)=5{t}^{2}-5$ and the subspace spanned by $q(t)=t+1$ with respect to the inner product." 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375658 1072 #uuid "ec6ca611-cb70-5ddf-8aeb-37ae54b275e7" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375658 1074 "The orthogonal projection of $p(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{q(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) & =\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1)}{q(-1)q(-1)+q(0)q(0)+q(1)q(1)}\\cdot q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\sum }_{i\\in {-1,0,1}}(5{t}^{2}-5){|}_{t=i}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{t=i}}{{\\sum }_{i\\in {-1,0,1}}(t+1){|}_{t=i}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{t=i}}\\cdot (t+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot 0+(-5)\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 2}{0\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 2}\\cdot (t+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{-5}{5}\\cdot (t+1) \\\\  & =-t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe distance between $p(t)=5{t}^{2}-5$ and the subspace spanned by $q(t)=t+1$ with respect to our inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖p(t)-{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖5{t}^{2}-5-(-t-1)‖ \\\\  & =‖5{t}^{2}+t-4‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨5{t}^{2}+t-4,5{t}^{2}+t-4⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨5{t}^{2}+t-4,5{t}^{2}+t-4⟩ & ={\\sum }_{i\\in {-1,0,1}}(5{t}^{2}+t-4){|}_{t=i}\\cdot (5{t}^{2}+t-4){|}_{t=i} \\\\  & =(0)\\cdot (0)+(-4)\\cdot (-4)+2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =0+16+4 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d=\\sqrt{20}.$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375659 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375660 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375659 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375661 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375659 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375662 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375659 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375663 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375659 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375664 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375659 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375659 1077 "selection" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375659 1079 #edb/ref 17592186375664 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375659 1080 #uuid "b5eb4129-4179-5cad-b537-5964562a2c96" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375660 1081 "\\sqrt{10}" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375660 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375660 1084 #uuid "69984854-c740-507d-a2b8-fdb5e8a42fe6" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375661 1081 "\\sqrt{30}" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375661 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375661 1084 #uuid "65e90a69-6ff8-5d0d-ac71-6f870d3f72c7" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375662 1081 "\\sqrt{40}" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375662 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375662 1084 #uuid "19d6b488-912b-552c-9eb4-cfe91a62376d" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375663 1081 "\\sqrt{50}" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375663 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375663 1084 #uuid "d3c16f29-9e9a-56f7-987c-bcfe8d3996cf" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375664 1081 "\\sqrt{20}" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375664 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375664 1084 #uuid "b69f5f8e-7f81-5690-b8cf-f05a442074d8" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375665 1068 #edb/ref 17592186375666 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375665 1069 "q-105790" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375665 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375665 1071 "Consider the matrix $L$ and the vector $b,$ given by \n\n$$\nL=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU factorization of some $3\\times 3$ matrix $A,$ find the vector $y$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $Ax=b.$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375665 1072 #uuid "f222a20b-2bd2-5f36-87f7-762e6e1190b5" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375665 1074 "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=5 \\\\ -2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=2 \\\\ 7{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}+{y}_{3}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the solution to this system using forward substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =5, \\\\ {y}_{2} & =2+2{y}_{1} \\\\  & =2+2(5) \\\\  & =12, \\\\ {y}_{3} & =-1-7{y}_{1}-{y}_{2} \\\\  & =-1-7(5)-(12) \\\\  & =-48.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$y=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 12 \\\\ -48\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375666 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375667 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375666 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375668 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375666 1075 #edb/ref 175921863