6045585 true] [17592186375672 1068 #edb/ref 17592186375673 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375672 1069 "q-106411" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375672 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375672 1071 "![](images/3f/3f1e86d5eebc6e36b4e4087dbdead613f45be89936651f529797c3faa331e99b.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375672 1072 #uuid "855fb4bb-64dc-5358-8f76-342d961ee64f" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375672 1074 "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(6,9).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(6,9),$ we can substitute $x=6$ and $y=9$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}6=2pt \\\\ 9=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =2pt \\\\ 3 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{3}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9 & =p{(\\frac{3}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 9 & =p\\cdot \\frac{9}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 9 & =\\frac{9}{p} \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(1)t,\\,y=(1){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=2t,\\,y={t}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375673 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375674 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375673 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375675 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375673 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375676 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375673 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375677 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [175921863756