\frac{1}{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =f(\\frac{1}{3})+{f}^{′}(\\frac{1}{3})(x-\\frac{1}{3})+\\frac{{f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{3})}{2}{(x-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of ${(x-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}$ in the above Taylor polynomial is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{3})}{2}.\n$$\n\nTo find this coefficient, we start by computing the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x) \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x) \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-4{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=\\frac{1}{3}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{3})=-4{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=-4{\\pi }^{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})=2{\\pi }^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine the coefficient of ${(x-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{3})}{2} & =\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{2}={\\pi }^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375825 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375826 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375825 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375827 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375825 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375828 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375825 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375829 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375825 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375830 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375825 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375825 1077 "selection" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375825 1079 #edb/ref 17592186375830 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375825 1080 #uuid "5ea1ff18-caf6-5b75-8df3-01bef44ceaad" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375826 1081 "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375826 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375826 1084 #uuid "5e8179dc-3fed-5f3f-84c9-45a6ccd780