)=⟨{e}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0,\\,2,\\,-{e}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0⟩=⟨0,2,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(0)$ and the principal normal $N(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0)\\times N(0) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,-1⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our perpendicular vector $n$ is \n\n$$\nn=T(0)\\times N(0)=⟨0,-1,0⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ denote the position vector of a point on the osculating plane. Since the plane is perpendicular to $n$ and passes through the tip of the position vector $p$, its Cartesian equation can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(⟨x,y,z⟩-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,-1,0⟩ & =⟨0,2,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨0,-1,0⟩ \\\\ x(0)+y(-1)+z(0) & =0(0)+2(-1)+(-1)(0) \\\\ 0-y+0 & =0-2+0 \\\\ y-2 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375867 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375868 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375867 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375869 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375867 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375870 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375867 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375871 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375867 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375872 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375867 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375867 1077 "selection" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375867 1079 #edb/ref 17592186375868 