aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}?\n$$\n\n- $\\text{rank}(A)=3$\n- $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5}$\n- $A$ is  invertible" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375978 1072 #uuid "017db163-2592-561e-a975-d762dc265a45" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375978 1074 "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\n \nwe have that $\\text{nullity}(A)=2.$\n\nNow,  for the matrix $A,$ the number of columns is $n=5,$ so according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+2 & =5 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =5-2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{nullity}(A)=2\\ne 0,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{5}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\text{rank}(A)=3\\ne 5$, according to the  invertible matrix theorem $A$ can't be an invertible matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375979 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375980 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375979 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375981 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375979 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375982 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186375979 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375983 1319413953