e] [17592186376051 1081 "I, II, and III only" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376051 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376051 1084 #uuid "8c102d18-68ae-569a-88c9-f7a90ad5be2d" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376052 1081 "IV only" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376052 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376052 1084 #uuid "3bb7d32d-49b3-5fe0-94cf-c277de74c950" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376053 1068 #edb/ref 17592186376054 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376053 1069 "q-156681" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376053 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376053 1071 "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $z=x+y$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $Π$ contains the zero-vector\n- $Π$ is closed under vector addition\n- $Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $Π$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376053 1072 #uuid "77f2cd70-5c51-5abc-8136-b13970866790" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376053 1074 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ x+y\\end{aligned}],\\,x,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $z=x+y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x+y \\\\ (0) & =(0)+(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}+{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{1}+{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}+{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{1}+{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ ({a}_{1}+{a}_{2})+({b}_{1}+{b}_{2})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ ({a}_{1}+{b}_{1})+({a}_{2}+{b}_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=x+y.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}+{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}+{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ k({a}_{1}+{a}_{2})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ k{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=x+y.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are true, the plane $Π$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\"" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376054 1075 #edb/ref 17592186376055 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376054 1075 #edb/ref 17592186376056 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376054 1075 #edb/ref 17592186376057 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376054 1075 #edb/ref 17592186376058 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376054 1075 #edb/ref 17592186376059 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376054 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376054 1077 "selection" 13194139533452 17592186045585 t