[17592186376420 1068 #edb/ref 17592186376490 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376420 1069 "q-182823" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376420 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376420 1071 "Use mathematical induction to prove that ${4}^{2n}-{10}^{n}$ is divisible by $6$ for all $n\\in N.$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376420 1072 #uuid "31d7e31a-275f-562a-8edf-8ff846d37000" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376420 1074 "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1),P(2),P(6),…,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$P(n):\\,6\\,∣\\,{4}^{2n}-{10}^{n}$ for all $n\\in N$\n\nWe start by proving the base case, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into the expression and show it is divisible by $6:$\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$ Substituting $n=1$ into the expression ${4}^{2n}-{10}^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\nP(1):\\,{4}^{2(1)}-{10}^{1}=6,\\,\\text{and}\\,6\\,∣\\,6.\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$\n\nDue to the base case, we already know that the statement is true for some value of $k,$ namely $k=1,$ so this assumption is valid.\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have that\n\n$$\nP(k):\\,6\\,∣\\,{4}^{2k}-{10}^{k}.\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\nP(k+1):\\,6\\,∣\\,{4}^{2(k+1)}-{10}^{k+1}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the expression ${4}^{2(k+1)}-{10}^{k+1}$ as ${4}^{2k}-{10}^{k}$ (taken from the inductive hypothesis) plus a multiple of $6.$ We do this as follows:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{2(k+1)}-{10}^{k+1} & ={4}^{2k}\\cdot {4}^{2}-{10}^{k}\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =16({4}^{2k})-10({10}^{k}) \\\\  & =(6+10)({4}^{2k})-10({10}^{k}) \\\\  & =6({4}^{2k})+10({4}^{2k})-10({10}^{k}) \\\\  & =6({4}^{2k})+10({4}^{2k}-{10}^{k})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $6\\,∣\\,({4}^{2k}-{10}^{k})$ by the inductive hypothesis, and $6({4}^{2k})$ is clearly divisible by $6,$ their sum is also divisible by $6,$ as required.\n\nSince we have shown that the expression in $(∗)$ is divisible by $6,$ our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, $6\\,∣\\,{4}^{2n}-{10}^{n}$ for all $n\\in N.$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376421 1075 #edb/ref 17592186376422 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376421 1075 #edb/ref 17592186376423 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376421 1075 #edb/ref 17592186376424 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376421 1075 #edb/ref 17592186376425 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376421 1075 #edb/ref 17592186376426 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376421 1075 #edb/ref 17592186376427 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376421 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376421 1077 "field-11" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376421 1079 #edb/ref 17592186376425 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376421 1080 #uuid "dc58e93f-ec14-5bc6-bfa0-86d5530e43e8" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376422 1081 "12({4}^{2k})" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376422 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376422 1084 #uuid "0e1b1a36-04a4-52e7-af01-41b29013fba7" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376423 1081 "12({4}^{2k+1})" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376423 108