 $|{a}_{n}-L|<ε$ for $n\\ge 2:$\n\nSolving this inequality for $n,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21} & <ε \\\\ 4 & <ε(9{n}^{2}-21) \\\\ \\frac{4}{ε} & <9{n}^{2}-21 \\\\ \\frac{4}{ε}+21 & <9{n}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{4}{9ε}+\\frac{7}{3} & <{n}^{2} \\\\ n & >\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{4}{9ε}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, if we want $|\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}-(-\\frac{1}{3})|<ε,$ it's sufficient to take all values of $n$ that are larger than $\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{4}{9ε}}.$\n\nWe can now continue the proof:\n\nTherefore, we choose $N>\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{4}{9ε}}.$\n\nNotice that, since $\\frac{4}{9ε}>0,$ then $\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{4}{9ε}}>\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}}≈1.53$ and therefore $N\\ge 2.$\n\nWith that in mind, we'll find the upper bound for the absolute value of the sequence's $n$th term:\n\nThen, for any natural number $n\\ge N,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}-(-\\frac{1}{3})| & =\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21} \\\\  & <\\frac{4}{9{(\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{4}{9ε}})}^{2}-21} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(21+\\frac{4}{ε}-21)} \\\\  & =ε.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make the conclusion:\n\nTherefore, according to the definition, ${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}=-\\frac{1}{3}.$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376559 1075 #edb/ref 17592186376560 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376559 1075 #edb/ref 17592186376561 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376559 1075 #edb/ref 17592186376562 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376559 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376559 1077 "field-7" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376559 1079 #edb/ref 17592186376562 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376559 1080 #uuid "3498dfaa-de46-5c8a-b515-f3e953cd5eae" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376560 1081 ">" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376560 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 1319413953