4139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376708 1084 #uuid "db568d93-3875-5af7-9432-a4e7975bebfa" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376709 1081 "-13" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376709 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376709 1084 #uuid "44b5b96e-b3e3-5a2a-b3fa-2d5714bb4b27" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376710 1068 #edb/ref 17592186376711 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376710 1069 "q-214364" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376710 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376710 1071 "Compute the result of the following operations on elements of ${Z}_{8}.$ Use the smallest nonnegative representative for the resulting class.\n\n$[\\,10\\,]\\cdot ([\\,3\\,]-[\\,6\\,])=[$\n{{field-1}}\n$]$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376710 1072 #uuid "2df001fb-6365-5403-9f02-465d2744e0c4" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376710 1074 "Addition, subtraction, and multiplication of integers in ${Z}_{n}$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,a\\,]\\pm [\\,b\\,] & =[\\,a\\pm b\\,] \\\\ [\\,a\\,]\\cdot [\\,b\\,] & =[\\,a\\cdot b\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that we can pick any element of an equivalence class as its representative. \n\nIn this case, all operations are carried out modulo $8.$\n\nWith that in mind, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,10\\,]\\cdot ([\\,3\\,]-[\\,6\\,]) & =[\\,10\\,]\\cdot [\\,3-6\\,] \\\\  & =[\\,10\\,]\\cdot [\\,-3\\,] \\\\  & =[\\,10\\,]\\cdot [\\,0-3\\,] \\\\  & =[\\,10\\,]\\cdot [\\,8-3\\,] \\\\  & =[\\,10\\,]\\cdot [\\,5\\,] \\\\  & =[\\,8+2\\,]\\cdot [\\,5\\,] \\\\  & =[\\,0+2\\,]\\cdot [\\,5\\,] \\\\  & =[\\,2\\,]\\cdot [\\,5\\,] \\\\  & =[\\,2\\cdot 5\\,] \\\\  & =[\\,10\\,] \\\\  & =[\\,8+2\\,] \\\\  & =[\\,2\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376711 1075 #edb/ref 17592186376712 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376711 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376711 1077 "field-1" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376711 1079 #edb/ref 17592186376712 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376711 1080 #uuid "2a42523a-e8bd-592b-a2af-4622623a55f1" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376712 1081 "2" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376712 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376712 1084 #uuid "2f7b9389-b16b-558c-9331-7796e118e6d1" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376713 1068 #edb/ref 17592186376714 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376713 1069 "q-216886" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376713 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376713 1071 "Which of the following relations are functional?\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x={y}^{2},$ defined on $R$\n\n- $x\\,S\\,y$ if and only if $x{y}^{3}=1,$ defined on ${R}^{+}$\n\n- $x\\,T\\,y$ if and only if $y∣2x,$ defined on $Z$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376713 1072 #uuid "bd2e7ad1-df50-507b-b322-c68735f660cf" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376713 1074 "A binary relation $R$ defined over $A$ and $B$ is *functional* (or a *partial function*) if, for all $a\\in A$ and ${b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\in B,$ we have \n\n$$\n(a,{b}_{1})\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(a,{b}_{2})\\in R\\,⇒\\,{b}_{1}={b}_{2}.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the first components of two ordered pairs are equal, then the second components must also be equal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations.\n\n- $R$ is *not* functional. Notice that $1={1}^{2}$ and $1=(-1)^{2}.$ So, $R$ contains pairs $(1,1)$ and $(1,-1)$ with the same first component but different second components.\n\n- $S$ is functional. Indeed, $a{b}_{1}^{3}=1$ and $a{b}_{2}^{3}=1$ is equivalent to ${b}_{1}={b}_{2}$ over ${R}^{+}.$ So, when the first components of two pairs are equal, then the second components are also equal.\n\n- $T$ is *not* functional. Notice that $2∣2(3)$ and $3∣2(3).$ So, $T$ contains pairs $(3,2)$ and $(3,3)$ with the same first component but different second components.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$S$ only.\"" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376714 1075 #edb/ref 17592186376715 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376714 1075 #edb/ref 17592186376716 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376714 1075 #edb/ref 17592186376717 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376714 1075 #edb/ref 17592186376718 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376714 1075 #edb/ref 17592186376719 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376714 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376714 1077 "selection" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376714 1079 #edb/ref 17592186376715 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376714 1080 #uuid "1d458a17-ac4e-55a1-8937-54fa569d2d12" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376715 1081 "$S$ only" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376715 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376715 1084 #uuid "56dc2b91-fa08-52f1-b205-fc89397886b2" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376716 1081 "$R$ and $S$  only" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376716 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376716 1084 #uuid "d19dbafd-3cd5-5f7c-a71b-f6d0068ca02a" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376717 1081 "$T$  only" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376717 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376717 1084 #uuid "f9a312b3-8689-58cc-8ccb-dc7b547a534a" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376718 1081 "$S$ and $T$  only" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376718 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376718 1084 #uuid "87b475a8-324e-5f0d-bfb1-9f13d14a996e" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376719 1081 "$R$  only" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376719 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376719 1084 #uuid "353fc6d0-0627-5629-9d53-796e92699492" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376720 1068 #edb/ref 17592186376721 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376720 1068 #edb/ref 17592186376725 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376720 1068 #edb/ref 17592186376728 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376720 1068 #edb/ref 17592186376731 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376720 1068 #edb/ref 17592186376735 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376720 1068 #edb/ref 17592186376738 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376720 1068 #edb/ref 17592186376741 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376720 1068 #edb/ref 17592186376744 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376720 1068 #edb/ref 17592186376747 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376720 1068 #edb/ref 17592186376751 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376720 1068 #edb/ref 17592186376755 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376720 1068 #edb/ref 17592186376759 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376720 1068 #edb/ref 17592186376762 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376720 1068 #edb/ref 17592186376765 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376720 1068 #edb/ref 17592186376768 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376720 1068 #edb/ref 17592186376771 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376720 1069 "q-218678" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376720 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376720 1071 "Prove that the function $f:C\\times C\\to C\\times C$ defined by $f(z,w)=(\\overset{―}{z-w},z\\text{i}+w)$ is injective." 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376720 1072 #uuid "255b0bd4-66a9-5482-bde6-26fd290eb0aa" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376720 1074 "First, we recall the definition of an injection:\n\nA function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if every element $x\\in X$ is mapped to a distinct element in $Y:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\ne {x}_{2}\\,⇒\\,f({x}_{1})\\ne f({x}_{2})\n$$\n\nIn this case, it would be easier to prove the contrapositive statement. \n\nThis implication is equivalent to its contrapositive:\n\n$$\nf({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2})\\,⇒\\,{x}_{1}={x}_{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we simply need to derive ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$ by assuming that $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ where ${x}_{1}=({z}_{1},{w}_{1})$ and ${x}_{2}=({z}_{2},{w}_{2}):$\n\nSo, let $({z}_{1},{w}_{1}),({z}_{2},{w}_{2})\\in C\\times C,$ and suppose that $f({z}_{1},{w}_{1})=f({z}_{2},{w}_{2}).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({z}_{1},{w}_{1}) & =f({z}_{2},{w}_{2}) \\\\ (\\overset{―}{{z}_{1}-{w}_{1}},{z}_{1}\\text{i}+{w}_{1}) & =(\\overset{―}{{z}_{2}-{w}_{2}},{z}_{2}\\text{i}+{w}_{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince two pairs are equal if and only if their respective components are equal, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{z}_{1}-{w}_{1}}=\\overset{―}{{z}_{2}-{w}_{2}} \\\\ {z}_{1}\\text{i}+{w}_{1}={z}_{2}\\text{i}+{w}_{2}\\end{aligned} & \\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}-{w}_{1}={z}_{2}-{w}_{2} \\\\ {z}_{1}\\text{i}+{w}_{1}={z}_{2}\\text{i}+{w}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\,⇒\\,{z}_{1}+{z}_{1}\\text{i}={z}_{2}+{z}_{2}\\text{i} \\\\  & \\,⇒\\,{z}_{1}(1+\\text{i})={z}_{2}(1+\\text{i}) \\\\  & \\,⇒\\,{z}_{1}={z}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}\\text{i}+{w}_{1} & ={z}_{1}\\text{i}+{w}_{2} \\\\ {w}_{1} & ={w}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we state the conclusion:\n\nHence, $({z}_{1},{w}_{1})=({z}_{2},{w}_{2}),$ as was to be shown. Therefore, $f$ is an injection." 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376721 1075 #edb/ref 17592186376722 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376721 1075 #edb/ref 1759218637672