ish to make use of the following result:* \n\n$$\n2\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot 1=\\text{gcd}(2,5)=1\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376774 1072 #uuid "fe0ed04b-4c73-5215-ab6a-71900e99c6cf" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376774 1074 "The connector \"if and only if\" means we must prove an equivalence. \n\n$$\nP⇔Q\n$$\n\nHowever, instead of proving an equivalence directly, we can instead prove two conditional statements. \n\n$$\nP⇔Q≡(P⇒Q)∧(Q⇒P)\n$$\n\nSo, we start our proof as follows:\n\nWe need to show  the following:\n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10)\\,⇔\\,(a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)\\,∧\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5))\n$$\n\nThis is the same as proving two implications.\n\nFirst, we consider the \"left to right\" implication.\n\n** Case $⇒:$** We need to show that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10)\\,⇒\\,(a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)\\,∧\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)).\n$$\n\nTo do that, we first write the statement in terms of divisibility. \n\nOr, in terms of divisibility,\n\n$$\n10∣a-b\\,⇒\\,(2∣a-b\\,∧\\,5∣a-b).\n$$\n\nThen, we write $n=a-b$ to simplify the notation. \n\nLet's denote $n=a-b.$\n\nSo, we now need to prove that \n\n$$\n10∣n\\,⇒\\,(2∣n\\,∧\\,5∣n).\n$$\n\nWe proceed as follows:\n\nIf $10∣n,$ then $n=10k$ for some integer $k.$ We can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\nn=2(5k),\n$$\n\nwhich means that $2∣n.$ Also, we can write\n\n$$\nn=5(2k),\n$$\n\nwhich means that $5∣n.$ Therefore, $2∣n$ and $5∣n.$\n\nNext, we consider the \"right to left\" implication.\n\n**Case $⇐:$** We need to show that\n\n$$\n(a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)\\,∧\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5))\\,⇒\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10).\n$$\n\nOr, in terms of divisibility,\n\n$$\n(2∣a-b\\,∧\\,5∣a-b)\\,⇒\\,10∣a-b.\n$$\n\nAgain, we simplify the notation by introducing $n=a-b.$ \n\nLet's again denote $n=a-b.$\n\nSo now, we need to prove\n\n$$\n(2∣n\\,∧\\,5∣n)\\,⇒\\,10∣n.\n$$\n\nNext, we use the definition of divisibility.\n\nSince $2∣n,$ then $n=2s$ for some integer $s,$ and since $5∣n,$ then $n=5t$ for some integer $t.$\n\nNotice that the divisibility of $n$ by $2$ and $5$ are independent. So, in general, we should use different letters ($s$ and $t$) in the reasoning above. \n\nNext, we'll use the fact that our divisors are coprime.\n\nThe integers $2$ and $5$ are coprime and $2\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot 1=1.$ So, multiplying this equation by $n,$ we get\n\n$$\n2\\cdot (-2n)+5\\cdot n=n.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $n=5t$ into the first term on the left-hand side and $n=2s$ into the second term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot (-2(5t))+5\\cdot (2s)=n \\\\ 10\\cdot (-2t)+10\\cdot (s)=n \\\\ 10\\cdot (-2t+s)=n,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that $10∣n.$ So, the second implication is proved.\n\nFinally, we write down the conclusion:\n\nTherefore, $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10)$ if and only if $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)$ and $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5).$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376775 1075 #edb/ref 17592186376776 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376775 1075 #edb/ref 17592186376777 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376775 1075 #edb/ref 17592186376778 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376775 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376775 1077 "field-23" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376775 1079 #edb/ref 17592186376778 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376775 1080 #uuid "97d1cb89-ea96-5315-bfb2-62f493c3303f" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376776 1081 "-n" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376776 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376776 1084 #uuid "5e5177d9-0147-5243-a4ae-f4c4052902a7" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376777 1081 "-5n" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376777 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376777 1084 #uuid "78d8b0fe-1399-5133-8311-c115c26c4499" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376778 1081 "-2n" 13194139533452 17