376986 1071 "![](images/95/953fcd84058c4c511386a907e806949b107dd8a1c19e1b8cafaae6ee52fd971f.png)\n\nThe value of the $x$-intercept of the parabola shown above is{{field-1}}." 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376986 1072 #uuid "2981c3bd-92be-569a-a7b3-e537ad2a1c94" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376986 1074 "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-4,-3)$ and passes through the point $(-8,1).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-4,-3)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y+3)^{2}=4p(x+4).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-8,1),$ we substitute $x=-8$ and $y=1$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1+3)^{2} & =4p(-8+4) \\\\ (4)^{2} & =4p\\cdot (-4) \\\\ 16 & =-16p \\\\ p & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =-4(x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0+3)^{2} & =-4(x+4) \\\\ 9 & =-4(x+4) \\\\ -\\frac{9}{4} & =x+4 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{25}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $-\\frac{25}{4}.$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376987 1075 #edb/ref 17592186376988 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376987 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376987 1077 "field-1" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376987 1079 #edb/ref 17592186376988 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376987 1080 #uuid "d61b4eae-e2e5-59c3-8e0e-8a4450c72d5a" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376988 1081 "-\\frac{25}{4}" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376988 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376988 1084 #uuid "e91ab584-1da0-5860-b0fe-dac2c86edd5c" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376989 1068 #edb/ref 17592186376990 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376989 1069 "q-251694" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376989 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376989 1071 "![](images/7e/7e3d4078888350baccee76e73ab9b4a80d2ec95359fbe965bef3dd6a07e5c3b5.png)\n\nThe value of the $x$-intercept of the parabola shown above is{{field-1}}." 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376989 1072 #uuid "7ed7cc33-cf3b-5b34-a2ab-03c46db15415" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376989 1074 "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-6,4)$ and passes through the point $(-4,8).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-6,4)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y-4)^{2}=4p(x+6).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-4,8),$ we substitute $x=-4$ and $y=8$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(8-4)^{2} & =4p(-4+6) \\\\ (4)^{2} & =4p\\cdot (2) \\\\ 16 & =8p \\\\ p & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =8(x+6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-4)^{2} & =8(x+6) \\\\ 16 & =8(x+6) \\\\ 2 & =x+6 \\\\ x & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $-4.$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376990 1075 #edb/ref 17592186376991 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376990 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376990 1077 "field-1" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376990 1079 #edb/ref 17592186376991 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376990 1080 #uuid "44eeaa7e-95d0-594d-b1b9-89222d742f44" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376991 1081 "-4" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376991 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376991 1084 #uuid "b18a44d7-d4b0-55cb-86af-0e13704b8df9" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376992 1068 #edb/ref 17592186376993 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376992 1069 "q-251697" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376992 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376992 1071 "![](images/fd/fd628bef568023d079cff41ed68efae949a0259822d79273671f633036e743ca.png)\n\nThe value of the $x$-intercept of the parabola shown above is{{field-1}}." 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376992 1072 #uuid "05525262-11b5-5c22-9660-edbf983c26e9" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376992 1074 "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(3,2)$ and passes through the point $(21,-4).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(3,2)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n{(y-2)}^{2}=4p(x-3).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(21,-4),$ we substitute $x=21$ and $y=-4$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(-4-2)}^{2} & =4p(21-3) \\\\ {(-6)}^{2} & =4p\\cdot (18) \\\\ 36 & =72p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =2(x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-2)^{2} & =2(x-3) \\\\ 4 & =2(x-3) \\\\ 2 & =x-3 \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $5.$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376993 1075 #edb/ref 17592186376994 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376993 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376993 1077 "field-1" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376993 1079 #edb/ref 17592186376994 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376993 1080 #uuid "e3a3dacb-4323-51ba-b4c3-d89cd23af82b" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376994 1081 "5" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376994 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376994 1084 #uuid "2b4f84fb-e01c-5de9-8a75-f717912ae5de" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376995 1068 #edb/ref 17592186376996 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376995 1068 #edb/ref 17592186376998 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376995 1069 "q-251792" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376995 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376995 1071 "![](images/10/1089d90610c132b5814a25d35f5bf8b9c3b6091aca6d64cd3e2379031096419b.png)\n\n**From left to right**, the **exact values** of the $x$-intercepts of the parabola shown above are{{field-1}}\nand \n{{field-2}}." 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376995 1072 #uuid "24d5ab01-a5ef-5e63-a7cd-e20c1c3c6797" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376995 1074 "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(1,2)$ and passes through the point $(3,-2).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(1,2)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =4p(y-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(3,-2),$ we substitute $x=3$ and $y=-2$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3-1)^{2} & =4p(-2-2) \\\\ 4 & =4p(-4) \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =-(y-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =-(0-2) \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =2 \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{2} \\\\ x & =1\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the $x$-intercepts are $1-\\sqrt{2}$  and $1+\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376996 1075 #edb/ref 17592186376997 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376996 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376996 1077 "field-2" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376996 1079 #edb/ref 17592186376997 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376996 1080 #uuid "fc35ec08-63bd-5fad-871e-df7c3efe54a1" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376997 1081 "1+\\sqrt{2}" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376997 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376997 1084 #uuid "9769691c-b769-53a3-96e0-25b6e7c41236" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376998 1075 #edb/ref 17592186376999 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376998 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376998 1077 "field-1" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376998 1079 #edb/ref 17592186376999 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376998 1080 #uuid "0f537715-609d-5c6f-af07-464eaff3e873" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376999 1081 "1-\\sqrt{2}" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376999 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186376999 1084 #uuid "9a902294-662a-515f-aae7-d86e2d47d37c" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377000 1068 #edb/ref 17592186377001 13194139533452 1759