}=15y.$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377065 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377066 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377065 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377065 1077 "field-1" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377065 1079 #edb/ref 17592186377066 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377065 1080 #uuid "c4d93942-00ee-55dd-a934-76b42e4cd40e" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377066 1081 "15y" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377066 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377066 1084 #uuid "4724059b-0d7a-5584-9ca7-fbfc9e9112f7" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377067 1068 #edb/ref 17592186377068 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377067 1069 "q-280364" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377067 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377067 1071 "Consider the following parabola:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{3}t,\\,y=\\frac{2}{9}{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the parabola is\n\n${x}^{2}=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377067 1072 #uuid "c95db2ce-954d-5074-ada9-b4a8ed2f22ff" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377067 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =3x,\\,{t}^{2}=\\frac{9y}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =9{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{9y}{2}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2} & =\\frac{9y}{2} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is ${x}^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}y.$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377068 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377069 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377068 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377068 1077 "field-1" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377068 1079 #edb/ref 17592186377069 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377068 1080 #uuid "ea7f74f9-1f1b-5e51-b0a2-7365c05a3847" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377069 1081 "\\frac{1}{2}y" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377069 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377069 1084 #uuid "09dd329f-7b97-541e-a30a-49a732186d21" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377070 1068 #edb/ref 17592186377071 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377070 1069 "q-280366" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377070 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377070 1071 "Consider the following parabola:\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y=-\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the parabola is\n\n${y}^{2}=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377070 1072 #uuid "86daae70-f44a-567b-940b-7b0ee09a76eb" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377070 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =-4x,\\,t=-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =4{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-4x$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{2} & =-4x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is ${y}^{2}=-x.$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377071 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377072 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377071 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377071 1077 "field-1" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377071 1079 #edb/ref 17592186377072 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377071 1080 #uuid "de751fba-f5ab-5f37-91fb-117219b162d9" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377072 1081 "-x" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377072 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377072 1084 #uuid "83f46071-8b90-5d28-b038-575e8afb3d34" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377073 1068 #edb/ref 17592186377074 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377073 1069 "q-316251" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377073 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377073 1071 "Water is being poured into a storage tank at a rate of $5t$ liters per minute. If at time $t=0$ the tank already contains $40$ liters of water, write an expression for $V(t),$ the total volume of water in the tank at time $t,$ where $t$ is measured in minutes.\n\n$V(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377073 1072 #uuid "d1669262-044b-56c9-938a-8d7361fae453" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377073 1074 "Let $f(t)=5t$ represent the rate at which water enters the tank, and let $V(T)$ be the total volume of water in the tank after $T$ minutes.\n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nV(T)=V(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(T) & =V(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =40+{\\int }_{0}^{T}5t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={40+(\\frac{5{t}^{2}}{2})|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =40+\\frac{5{T}^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable  $t.$ We have\n\n$$\nV(t)=40+\\frac{5{t}^{2}}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377074 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377075 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377074 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377074 1077 "field-1" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377074 1079 #edb/ref 17592186377075 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377074 1080 #uuid "c60e8ff8-8e89-5160-a0d8-53fd5813b77e" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377075 1081 "40+\\frac{5{t}^{2}}{2}" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377075 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377075 1084 #uuid "dea13ba0-1a30-5294-9ce2-1654ed4bab1d" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377076 1068 #edb/ref 17592186377077 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377076 1069 "q-316260" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377076 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377076 1071 "![](images/5d/5d27ef86b95e75f0b97c54a4c6a4fc382fe06aefdaad7fb623ce34c5b2b88dbe.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of four line segments. Then,\n\n${\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377076 1072 #uuid "94b0c76a-b611-5c4e-b147-c5eb91ec97fa" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377076 1074 "The graph consists of four parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-5,-2],$ the line segment for $x\\in (-2,1],$ the line segment for $x\\in (1,3],$ and the line segment for $x\\in [3,4].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-5}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-2}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{4}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-5,-2].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-2,1].$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =bh \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-3.\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (1,3].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $4$th integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [3,4].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3}+{A}_{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}-3+2+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377077 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377078 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377077 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377077 1077 "field-1" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377077 1079 #edb/ref 17592186377078 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377077 1080 #uuid "99233dca-e526-56e1-8eaf-74d1dccfa626" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377078 1081 "4" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377078 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377078 1084 #uuid "b4e14d39-0706-5ade-baf1-149cca3be3d0" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377079 1068 #edb/ref 17592186377080 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377079 1069 "q-316823" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377079 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377079 1071 "![](images/71/71ec24e4ce1958f135e00f40a45b471f4f47748d5452f6ed4f2631dbaa0939c1.png)\n\nConsider the graph of a function $y=f(x),$ shown above. It consists of two line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-4}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377079 1072 #uuid "0d86fe8e-5986-550d-bf2b-f605a835ed60" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377079 1074 "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-4,1]$ and the line segment for $x\\in [1,5].$ Therefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,1].$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =lw \\\\  & =5\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,5].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =10+4 \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377080 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377081 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377080 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377080 1077 "field-1" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377080 1079 #edb/ref 17592186377081 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377080 1080 #uuid "f81a59b6-1c4f-5ac7-bf85-210e19fa8408" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377081 1081 "14" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377081 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377081 1084 #uuid "250f9cf3-e4c2-5e7e-8605-f8d4fcf5c883" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377082 1068 #edb/ref 17592186377083 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377082 1069 "q-317321" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377082 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377082 1071 "Let $a=⟨0,2,1⟩$, $b=⟨2,0,3⟩$ and $c=⟨1,4,5⟩.$ Find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using a determinant.\n\n$a\\cdot (b\\times c)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377082 1072 #uuid "77a13127-37ab-53ba-baec-4cca7e404b6d" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377082 1074 "Remember that the scalar triple product $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}|\\,-\\,2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}|\\,+\\,1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0\\,(0\\cdot 5-3\\cdot 4)\\,-\\,2\\,(2\\cdot 5-3\\cdot 1)\\,+\\,1\\,(2\\cdot 4-0\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =0(-12)\\,-\\,2\\,(7)\\,+\\,1\\,(8) \\\\  & =0-14+8 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377083 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377084 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377083 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377083 1077 "field-1" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377083 1079 #edb/ref 17592186377084 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377083 1080 #uuid "d1fb31f8-7ebd-50f5-9d92-ee287a39ccbe" 