⁡(2x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n7(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-14(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))=-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(14\\beta -14\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+(-14\\alpha -14\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}14\\beta -14\\alpha =-8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ -14\\alpha -14\\beta =3\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{5}{28},\\beta =-\\frac{11}{28}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{5}{28}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\frac{11}{28}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}+(\\frac{5}{28})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+(-\\frac{11}{28})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377139 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377140 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377139 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377139 1077 "field-1" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377139 1079 #edb/ref 17592186