n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)={z}^{′}(x)+{y}_{0}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, ${y}_{0}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Since ${y}_{0}^{′}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$, we have\n\n$$\ny=z+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,⇒\\,{y}^{′}={z}^{′}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's substitute these expressions into our Riccati equation:\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(z+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,(z+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+{sin}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the right-hand side, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{′}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =({sin}^{2}⁡x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,z+{z}^{2})-(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,z+2{sin}^{2}⁡x)+{sin}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {z}^{′}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & ={z}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {z}^{′} & ={z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve the obtained equation using separation of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {z}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{z} & =x+C \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{x+C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nFinally, let's substitute $z=y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ to recover the initial variable $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =-\\frac{1}{x+C} \\\\ y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-\\frac{1}{x+C} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{x+C}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{1}{x+C}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377186 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377187 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377186 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377188 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377186 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377189 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377186 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377190 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377186 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377191 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [175921