lowing initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+2{y}^{2},\\,y(0.5)=0\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(1).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377192 1072 #uuid "ea72c39d-1dce-5dd6-9f51-3c61cc1887f0" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377192 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-0.5=0.5.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((x+2{y}^{2})+({x}_{\\text{new}}+2({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}))\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.25x+0.5{y}^{2}+0.25{x}_{\\text{new}}+0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+0.25x+0.5{y}^{2}+0.25{x}_{\\text{new}}+0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-0.5{y}^{2}-0.25{x}_{\\text{new}}-0.25x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.5$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-0.5{y}^{2}-0.25{x}_{\\text{new}}-0.25x.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0.5+0.5=1.\n$$\n\nThen, using the initial condition $y(0.5)=0,$ we have $x=0.5$ and $y=0,$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-0-0.5(0)^{2}-0.25\\cdot 1-0.25\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.25-0.125 \\\\  & =-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.375.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=0$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}{{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})}.\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})=-({y}_{\\text{new}})+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{-0.5(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{2}+({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-0.375}{-({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}+1}.\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(0.5)=0,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =0 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & ≈0.375 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈0.4875 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈0.4998\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈0.50,$ rounded to two decimal places." 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377193 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377194 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377193 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377193 1077 "field-1" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377193 1079 #edb/ref 17592186377194 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377193 1080 #uuid "1ff65686-0ceb-5930-8a32-5705039e7de0" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377194 1081 "0.50" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377194 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377194 1084 #uuid "af63086c-5e61-56b5-8e5d-4d1162f7df69" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377195 1068 #edb/ref 17592186377196 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377195 1069 "q-334432" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377195 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377195 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x{y}^{2}-2,\\,y(0)=2.02\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(0.5).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(0.5)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377195 1072 #uuid "20d4acdc-dfe5-5c28-81bf-251b6364882f" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377195 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=0.5,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=0.5-0=0.5.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((x{y}^{2}-2)+({x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-2))\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x{y}^{2}+{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-4)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.25x{y}^{2}+0.25{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+0.25x{y}^{2}+0.25{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.25{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+0.25x{y}^{2}+y-1-{y}_{\\text{new}}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.5$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.25{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+0.25x{y}^{2}+y-1-{y}_{\\text{new}}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+0.5=0.5.\n$$\n\n Then, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.25(0.5)({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+0.25(0)(2.02)^{2}+2.02-1-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & =0.125({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1.02-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & =0.125({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}}+1.02.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=2.02$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}{{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})}.\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{0.125(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{2}-({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}+1.02}{0.25(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})-1}.\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(0)=2.02,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =2.02 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & ≈1.030\\,202 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈1.195\\,146 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈1.199\\,996\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(0.5)≈1.20,$ rounded to two decimal places." 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377196 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377197 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377196 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377196 1077 "field-1" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377196 1079 #edb/ref 17592186377197 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377196 1080 #uuid "f03ad0dc-28cd-5a62-970a-621c33e6e67d" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377197 1081 "1.20" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377197 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377197 1084 #uuid "ef577a71-488a-5fb1-af76-763a6f4af08e" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377198 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377199 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377198 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377200 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377198 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377201 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377198 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377202 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377198 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377203 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377198 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377198 1077 "selection" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377198 1079 #edb/ref 17592186377201 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377198 1080 #uuid "33da174c-337a-5186-96b2-62c3b16e65a3" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377199 1081 "201.0\\,\\text{lb}" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377199 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377199 1084 #uuid "1ac342bc-5d31-58a3-842a-8a4e79ae42dd" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377200 1081 "204.3\\,\\text{lb}" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377200 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377200 1084 #uuid "2980a552-e3d5-5564-ab0e-1bbb2358ce8f" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377201 1081 "207.6\\,\\text{lb}" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377201 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377201 1084 #uuid "b4d44c02-122b-57ce-ae8c-3073cccbf6f4" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377202 1081 "206.2\\,\\text{lb}" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377202 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377202 1084 #uuid "49a8983c-5113-540c-9960-edc50602eed7" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377203 1081 "205.4\\,\\text{lb}" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377203 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377203 1084 #uuid "8c1780f1-fe83-536d-9909-81d9b471da59" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377204 1068 #edb/ref 17592186377205 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377204 1069 "q-336506" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377204 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377204 1071 "A coolant reservoir in a manufacturing plant can hold up to $210\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $70\\,\\text{gal}$ of coolant solution with $20\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved corrosion inhibitor. A more concentrated coolant solution with concentration $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $21\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $14\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of inhibitor in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377204 1072 #uuid "c2fb266c-0e36-5075-b402-7e3b0f1013d7" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377204 1074 "Since $Q$ is the amount of inhibitor in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which inhibitor enters the reservoir minus the rate at which inhibitor leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which inhibitor enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of inhibitor enters the reservoir at a rate of $21\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *inhibitor* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (21\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (21\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =10.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which inhibitor exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which inhibitor leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $70\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $21\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $14\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=70+21t-14t=7(10+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of inhibitor in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{7(10+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $14\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *inhibitor* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(14\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{7(10+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(14\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{7(10+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{14Q}{7(10+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{10+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =10.5-\\frac{2Q}{10+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{10+t}\\,Q=10.5.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(10+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|10+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(10+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(10+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(10+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{10+t}\\,Q) & =(10+t)^{2}\\cdot 10.5 \\\\ (10+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(10+t)Q & =10.5(10+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(10+t)^{2}) & =10.5(10+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(10+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 10.5(10+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(10+t)^{2} & =10.5\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(10+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =3.5(10+t)+\\frac{C}{(10+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $20\\,\\text{lb}$ of inhibitor. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=20,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =20 \\\\ 3.5(10+0)+\\frac{C}{(10+0)^{2}} & =20 \\\\ 35+\\frac{C}{(10)^{2}} & =20 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{10}^{2}} & =-15 \\\\ C & =-15(10)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =3.5(10+t)-\\frac{15(10)^{2}}{(10+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =3.5(10+t)-15{(\\frac{10}{10+t})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $210\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=210$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =210 \\\\ 70+21{t}_{o}-14{t}_{o} & =210 \\\\ 70+7{t}_{o} & =210 \\\\ 7{t}_{o} & =140 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =20\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of inhibitor in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(20) & =3.5(10+20)-15{(\\frac{10}{10+20})}^{2} \\\\  & =105-15{(\\frac{10}{30})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈103.3\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377205 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377206 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377205 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377207 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377205 1075 #edb/ref 175921863