 current is\n{{field-1}} amperes." 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377225 1072 #uuid "baddbe2e-5062-58fc-a5b7-9bf88f202c4d" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377225 1074 "The equation is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+8{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${I}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0,$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+8\\alpha =6\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}+\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}=A{e}^{-8t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${I}_{p}=\\frac{3}{4}$ is the *steady-state current*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the current $I$ approaches the value of the steady-state current.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state current is $\\frac{3}{4}$ amperes." 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377226 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377227 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377226 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377226 1077 "field-1" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377226 1079 #edb/ref 17592186377227 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377226 1080 #uuid "8749cc96-0ec2-5518-bf4f-7c4b6f7a3aeb" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377227 1081 "0.75" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377227 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377227 1084 #uuid "b085f7b4-1f0e-56f8-a51f-0da8860bf7ef" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377228 1068 #edb/ref 17592186377229 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377228 1069 "q-336646" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377228 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377228 1071 "![](images/25/256c09422d242886f67b02abba2d2e8887b4dea1f45332dc57a106aabc2154f9.png)\n\nFind an equation for the current $I(t)$ (in amperes) in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) **only**, given that the current when $t=0\\,\\text{s}$ is $1\\,\\text{A}.$\n\n$I(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377228 1072 #uuid "1ae77f4e-88f7-5352-a9db-21c8f98ada40" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377228 1074 "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $4\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance of $20\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\cdot \\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n4=20I+2\\cdot \\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+10I=2.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+10{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-10t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${I}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+10\\alpha =2\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-10t}+\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial current of $1\\,\\text{A}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $I(0)=1,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(0) & =1 \\\\ A{e}^{-10(0)}+\\frac{1}{5} & =1 \\\\ A & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $I(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{4}{5}{e}^{-10t}+\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(4{e}^{-10t}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}=\\frac{4}{5}{e}^{-10t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${I}_{p}=\\frac{1}{5}$ is the *steady-state current*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the current $I$ approaches the value of the steady-state current." 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377229 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377230 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377229 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377229 1077 "field-1" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377229 1079 #edb/ref 17592186377230 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377229 1080 #uuid "e9777acc-bd37-5aea-a411-5ae6180e338a" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377230 1081 "(4{e}^{-10t}+1)/5" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377230 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377230 1084 #uuid "e9f27e85-ca05-5649-bf8e-533410517cd8" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377231 1068 #edb/ref 17592186377232 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377231 1069 "q-336652" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377231 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377231 1071 "A series RL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $30\\,Ω,$ and an inductance of $3\\,\\text{H}.$ Find, **rounded to two decimal places**, the circuit's current after $0.02$ seconds given that the initial current is $1\\,\\text{A}.$\n\nThe current after $0.02$ seconds is\n{{field-1}} amperes." 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377231 1072 #uuid "5c397fb9-bf38-5a44-8a25-4f5a4f9ab367" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377231 1074 "Kirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=30\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=30I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=3\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=3\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n6=30I+3\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+10I=2.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+10{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-10t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${I}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0,$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+10\\alpha =2\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-10t}+\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial current of $1\\,\\text{A}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $I(0)=1,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(0) & =1 \\\\ A{e}^{-10(0)}+\\frac{1}{5} & =1 \\\\ A & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $I(t):$\n\n$$\nI(t)=\\frac{4}{5}{e}^{-10t}+\\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{1}{5}(4{e}^{-10t}+1)\n$$\n\nFinally, the current in the circuit after $t=0.02$ seconds is\n\n$$\nI(0.02)=\\frac{1}{5}(4{e}^{-10(0.02)}+1)≈0.85\\,\\text{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377232 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377233 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377232 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377232 1077 "field-1" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377232 1079 #edb/ref 17592186377233 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377232 1080 #uuid "2791feb8-6710-5d48-88a6-a2f36341287f" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377233 1081 "0.85" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377233 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377233 1084 #uuid "b2deb0dc-4abd-518c-854a-8134786e852f" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377234 1068 #edb/ref 17592186377235 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377234 1069 "q-33666" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377234 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377234 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the origin and the osculating plane for the curve $r(t)$ at the point where $t=1,$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =6t\\,i+2{t}^{3}\\,j+3\\sqrt{2}{t}^{2}\\,k \\\\ T(t) & =\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}}(i+{t}^{2}\\,j+\\sqrt{2}tk) \\\\ N(t) & =\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}}(-\\sqrt{2}t\\,i+\\sqrt{2}t\\,j+(1-{t}^{2})k)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377234 1072 #uuid "32baf9a2-5dec-5cae-80bc-3e8a8d3f6b84" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377234 1074 "First, notice that the unit tangent and the principal normal at the point where $t=1$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =\\frac{1}{1+1}(i+1\\,j+\\sqrt{2}k)=\\frac{1}{2}(i+j+\\sqrt{2}k) \\\\ N(1) & =\\frac{1}{1+1}(-\\sqrt{2}\\,i+\\sqrt{2}\\,j+(1-1)k)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(-i+j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the position vector of the point  where $t=1$ is\n\n$$\np=r(1)=6\\,i+2\\,j+3\\sqrt{2}\\,k.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(1)$ and the principal normal $N(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =T(1)\\times N(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(i+j+\\sqrt{2}k)k\\times (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(-i+j)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}⟨-\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{2},2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨-1,-1,\\sqrt{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance between our plane and the origin is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{|(p-0)\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=\\frac{|p\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=|p\\cdot n|,\n$$\n\nsince $‖n‖=1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|p\\cdot n| \\\\  & =|⟨6,2,3\\sqrt{2}⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}⟨-1,-1,\\sqrt{2}⟩| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}|-6-2+6| \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377235 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377236 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377235 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377237 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377235 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377238 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377235 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377239 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377235 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377240 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377235 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377235 1077 "selection" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377235 1079 #edb/ref 17592186377239 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377235 1080 #uuid "81d7dedc-08dc-555b-8b66-861162a645bb" 13194139533452 17