tangent and principal normal, respectively." 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377241 1072 #uuid "bedcf41a-1f98-5cfb-aa75-b3495ee5c2e7" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377241 1074 "First, notice that the unit tangent and the principal normal at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}⟩=⟨-1,0,0⟩ \\\\ N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}⟩=-\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,3,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the position vector of the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is\n\n$$\np=r(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}⟩=⟨0,3,4⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and the principal normal $N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\times N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨-1,0,0⟩\\times (-\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,3,4⟩) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,4,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are interested only in the direction of the above vector, let's write our perpendicular vector $n$ as \n\n$$\nn=(-5)\\cdot (T(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\times N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))=⟨0,4,-3⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ denote the position vector of a point on the osculating plane. Since the plane is perpendicular to $n$ and passes through the tip of the position vector $p$, its Cartesian equation can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(⟨x,y,z⟩-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,4,-3⟩ & =⟨0,3,4⟩\\cdot ⟨0,4,-3⟩ \\\\ 0\\cdot x+4\\cdot y+(-3)\\cdot z & =0 \\\\ 4y-3z & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377242 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377243 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377242 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377244 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377242 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377245 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377242 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377246 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377242 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377247 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377242 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377242 1077 "selection" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377242 1079 #edb/ref 17592186377244 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377242 1080 #uuid "5dc189f4-275b-5dfe-b268-f73759792b0d" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377243 1081 "-3y+4z=0" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377243 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377243 1084 #uuid "51c4ccf1-eb7e-5b5f-a3cf-60eae080271f" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377244 1081 "4y-3z=0" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377244 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377244 1084 #uuid "ae591169-a058-56e3-b4b4-ac2b45753920" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377245 1081 "4x-3y=0" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377245 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377245 1084 #uuid "f8f71240-ae22-528e-99ab-71f715fa697c" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377246 1081 "-4y-3z=0" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377246 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377246 1084 #uuid "abd101c7-b915-5fd8-9dfe-6a323905a8ac" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377247 1081 "4x+3z=0" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377247 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377247 1084 #uuid "66040242-4691-579e-beba-f5503538cc0b" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377248 1068 #edb/ref 17592186377249 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377248 1069 "q-336734" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377248 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377248 1071 "A nutrient-mixing tank initially holds $720\\,\\text{L}$ of solution containing $3\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved supplement.\nA feed solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the supplement is pumped into the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of supplement in the tank $5$ minutes after pumping begins." 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377248 1072 #uuid "3ea5d5e7-af5c-553a-9742-7d54f2384984" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377248 1074 "Let $Q$ be the amount of supplement in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which supplements enter the tank minus the rate at which supplements leave the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which supplements enter the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the supplement enters the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *supplement* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.36\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which supplements exit the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $720\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of supplement in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{720}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min},$ *supplement* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(12\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{720}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{720}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12Q}{720}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{60}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.36-\\frac{Q}{60}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{60}Q=0.36.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{60}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/60},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{60}\\alpha =0.36\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =21.6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=21.6.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/60}+21.6.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $3\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved supplement. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=3,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =3 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/60}+21.6 & =3 \\\\ A & =-18.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=21.6-18.6{e}^{-t/60}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of supplement in the tank $5$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\nQ(5)=21.6-18.6{e}^{-5/60}≈4.49\\,\\text{kg},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377249 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377250 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377249 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377251 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377249 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377252 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377249 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377253 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377249 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377254 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377249 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377249 1077 "selection" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377249 1079 #edb/ref 17592186377252 13194139533452 1759218604