xed liquid leaves at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min},$ *disinfectant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{400}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{400}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{20Q}{400}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{20}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.6-\\frac{Q}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{20}Q=0.6.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{20}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/20},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{20}\\alpha =0.6\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =12.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=12.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/20}+12.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $3\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved disinfectant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=3,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =3 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/20}+12 & =3 \\\\ A & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=12-9{e}^{-t/20}.\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377270 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377271 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377270 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377272 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377270 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377273 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377270 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377274 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377270 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377275 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377270 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377270 1077 "selection" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377270 1079 #edb/ref 17592186377272 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377270 1080 #uuid "9149b0d8-97dc-543b-a8ef-8e274f2d81bc" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377271 1081 "Q(t)=10-7{e}^{t/20}" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377271 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377271 1084 #uuid "19d2fe13-097f-5e6f-9c33-2c32b0e27d8e" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377272 1081 "Q(t)=12-9{e}^{-t/20}" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377272 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377272 1084 #uuid "ed6b381a-a203-5376-a746-2de328b0ebe1" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377273 1081 "Q(t)=-1+4{e}^{t/20}" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377273 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377273 1084 #uuid "ffd3ac0f-6dcc-58e2-95b1-2de9c477d9b8" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377274 1081 "Q(t)=4-{e}^{-t/20}" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377274 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377274 1084 #uuid "864fcaca-7914-585c-ab8c-d6bf235cc102" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377275 1081 "Q(t)=-9+12{e}^{-t/20}" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377275 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377275 1084 #uuid "c03d0083-ace6-59b4-99bc-ac215dea614c" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377276 1068 #edb/ref 17592186377277 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377276 1069 "q-336815" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377276 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377276 1071 "![](images/97/97d661baf511c1e99ee8eed7fbed2ad02d9f96c452ece5b765a3e2cf657c431b.png)\n\nFind the amplitude of the steady-state current for the RL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\nThe amplitude of the steady-state current is approximately\n{{field-1}} amperes." 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377276 1072 #uuid "fcce442e-f4af-544c-929a-c36fe279cfc9" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377276 1074 "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $6\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=6\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=6I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=1\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=6I+\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+6{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-6t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))+6(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-3\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+(6\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha +6\\beta =10\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\ 6\\alpha +3\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{2}{3},$ $\\beta =\\frac{4}{3}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-6t}-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-6t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}}≈1.49\\,\\text{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377277 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377278 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377277 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377277 1077 "field-1" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377277 1079 #edb/ref 17592186377278 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377277 1080 #uuid "b7d1f7d0-ad3b-599f-aa11-6740016d6811" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377278 1081 "1.49" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377278 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377278 1084 #uuid "32e02ad9-f01e-5d91-b67e-c8ac9b2f9690" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377279 1068 #edb/ref 17592186377280 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377279 1069 "q-336817" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [1