(t)=A{e}^{-8t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))+8(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ (-2\\alpha +8\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+(8\\alpha +2\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha +8\\beta =8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ 8\\alpha +2\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{4}{17},$ $\\beta =\\frac{16}{17}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{4}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{16}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}-\\frac{4}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{16}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{4}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{16}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{4}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{16}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{4}{17})}^{2}+{(\\frac{16}{17})}^{2}}≈0.97\\,\\text{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377280 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377281 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377280 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377280 1077 "field-1" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377280 1079 #edb/ref 17592186377281 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377280 1080 #uuid "207cd688-1b68-5ccb-98fe-558f7b0217d8" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377281 1081 "0.97" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377281 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377281 1084 #uuid "56be3e3d-cc68-5b73-870d-17bf13a0d689" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377282 1068 #edb/ref 17592186377283 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377282 1069 "q-338480" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377282 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377282 1071 "![](images/d7/d755a1ddb893f070a088eddee611f56cd98eb550a8beecadc9bf8e514935cb6d.png)\n\nThe graph $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of three line segments. Then,\n\n${\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377282 1072 #uuid "73f3f6c1-8ae7-5724-84e0-31378df05c39" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377282 1074 "We divide the interval of integration into $4$ regions: the line segment for $x\\in [-5,-2],$ the line segment for $x\\in (-2,-1],$ the line segment for $x\\in (-1,1],$ and the line segment for $x\\in (1,4].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-5}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{3}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{4}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-5,-2].$    The corresponding region is a rectangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =bh \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-2,-1].$   The corresponding region is a triangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-1,1].$   The corresponding region is a triangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{3}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $4$th integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (1,4].$   The corresponding region is a rectangle below the $x$-axis, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{4} & =bh \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{4}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}-{A}_{3}-{A}_{4} \\\\  & =6+\\frac{1}{2}-2-3 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377283 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377284 13194139533452 1