 region is a quarter-circle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}+{A}_{3}+{A}_{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}+3+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}+\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377388 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377389 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377388 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377388 1077 "field-1" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377388 1079 #edb/ref 17592186377389 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377388 1080 #uuid "561b8ccd-1437-527e-b2de-3768a8b3c513" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377389 1081 "\\frac{7}{2}+\\frac{5\\pi }{4}" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377389 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377389 1084 #uuid "4b8ba649-d93b-5b7a-9924-339183f33559" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377390 1068 #edb/ref 17592186377391 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377390 1069 "q-345062" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377390 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377390 1071 "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n4x{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+5y=0\n$$\n\nLet $y={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x=0$ of the differential equation above.\nGiven that $\\lambda \\in (-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}),$ find the recurrence relation for the coefficients ${a}_{n},$ where $n=1,2,3,….$\n\n*Hint: Substituting the Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x{y}^{″} & =4({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\ 5y & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }5{a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377390 1072 #uuid "2fd5c8b0-45b5-5022-b14e-636dff9e0e7a" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377390 1074 "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain:\n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\nWe are given the three terms of the ODE as power series with the $n=0$ term isolated in each.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x{y}^{″} & =4({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\ 5y & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }5{a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we continue from this point.\n\n**Step 1**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1} & = \\\\ (4{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1} & = \\\\ (4{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}. & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[4((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}+(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}+5{a}_{n-1}]{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} & = \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda -1}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[4(\\lambda +n)^{2}{a}_{n}-3(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}+5{a}_{n-1}]{x}^{n} & = \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda -1}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[(4(\\lambda +n)^{2}-3(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}+5{a}_{n-1}]{x}^{n} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{x}^{\\lambda -1}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[(4(\\lambda +n)^{2}-3(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}+5{a}_{n-1}]{x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (4{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda ){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[(4(\\lambda +n)^{2}-3(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}+5{a}_{n-1}]{x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficients of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda ){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (4\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =0,\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we're interested in $\\lambda \\in (-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}),$ we take $\\lambda =0.$\n\nSimilarly, setting the coefficients of ${x}^{n}$ for $n\\ge 1$ equal to $0,$ we obtain the following recurrence relation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4(\\lambda +n)^{2}-3(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}+5{a}_{n-1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $\\lambda =0$ into the above and solve for ${a}_{n}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n(4n-3){a}_{n} & =-5{a}_{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{n} & =-\\frac{5{a}_{n-1}}{n(4n-3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377391 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377392 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377391 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377393 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377391 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377394 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377391 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377395 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377391 1075 