lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\ 4y & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4{a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377423 1072 #uuid "089c5bdc-f5a1-5a0f-b3c9-bb67da43b1ce" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377423 1074 "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain:\n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\nWe are given the three terms of the ODE as power series with the $n=0$ term isolated in each.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x{y}^{″} & =6({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\ 4y & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4{a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we continue from this point.\n\n**Step 1**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1} & = \\\\ (6{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1} & = \\\\ (6{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}. & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[6((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}+(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}+4{a}_{n-1}]{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} & = \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda -1}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[6(\\lambda +n)^{2}{a}_{n}-5(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}+4{a}_{n-1}]{x}^{n} & = \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda -1}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[(6(\\lambda +n)^{2}-5(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}+4{a}_{n-1}]{x}^{n} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{x}^{\\lambda -1}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[(6(\\lambda +n)^{2}-5(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}+4{a}_{n-1}]{x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (6{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda ){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[(6(\\lambda +n)^{2}-5(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}+4{a}_{n-1}]{x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficients of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda ){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (6\\lambda -5) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =0,\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we're interested in $\\lambda \\in (\\frac{1}{2},1),$ we take $\\lambda =\\frac{5}{6}.$\n\nSimilarly, setting the coefficients of ${x}^{n}$ for $n\\ge 1$ equal to $0,$ we obtain the following recurrence relation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6(\\lambda +n)^{2}-5(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}+4{a}_{n-1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $\\lambda =\\frac{5}{6}$ into the above and solve for ${a}_{n}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(n+\\frac{5}{6})(6(n+\\frac{5}{6})-5){a}_{n} & =-4{a}_{n-1} \\\\ (n+\\frac{5}{6})(6n){a}_{n} & =-4{a}_{n-1} \\\\ n(6n+5){a}_{n} & =-4{a}_{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{n} & =-\\frac{4{a}_{n-1}}{n(6n+5)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377424 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377425 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377424 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377426 13194139533452 17592186045