7592186377714 1074 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}=\\frac{{0}^{2}\\cdot 0}{{0}^{3}+{0}^{3}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths. \n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the line $y=2x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=2x}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}+8{x}^{3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377715 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377716 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377715 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377717 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377715 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377718 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377715 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377719 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377715 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377720 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377715 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377715 1077 "selection" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377715 1079 #edb/ref 17592186377719 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377715 1080 #uuid "f14349ec-0b87-5b7c-b87c-2368f3340f77" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377716 1081 "-\