 1069 "q-79298" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377748 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377748 1071 "![](images/02/02ed30bbb45715a9c33f2c7dad79ce2dd17dd065d96fbd6fbb46a1b366456df8.png)\n\nWhat are the $y$-intercepts of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377748 1072 #uuid "955f1829-97d4-59ca-9f7e-e493ecb2da78" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377748 1074 "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(9,1)$ and passes through the point $(-7,5).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(9,1)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y-1)^{2}=4p(x-9).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-7,5),$ we substitute $x=-7$ and $y=5$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5-1)^{2} & =4p(-7-9) \\\\ 16 & =4p(-16) \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-1)^{2} & =-(x-9).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-1)^{2} & =-(0-9) \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =9 \\\\ y-1 & =\\pm 3 \\\\ y & =-2,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercepts are $-2$ and $4.$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377749 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377750 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377749 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377751 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377749 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377752 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377749 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377753 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377749 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377754 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377749 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 1319413953