id "8ea80694-1e35-5138-9aa9-14406644ce5a" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377892 1068 #edb/ref 17592186377893 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377892 1069 "q-83343" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377892 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377892 1071 "Find the Laplace transform of $y(t),$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0.\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377892 1072 #uuid "3efcab48-854e-5d97-8dbf-5336356b2708" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377892 1074 "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the scalar multiplication property of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{″}} & =L{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\ L{{y}^{″}} & =2L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's introduce $L{y}=Y(s).$ \n\nTo calculate $L{{y}^{″}},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a second derivative:\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{″}}={s}^{2}L{y}-sy(0)-{y}^{′}(0)={s}^{2}Y(s)-sy(0)-{y}^{′}(0)\n$$\n\nTaking into account the given initial conditions, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{″}}={s}^{2}Y(s)-s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have the following result:\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{″}} & =2L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\ {s}^{2}Y(s)-s & =\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ {s}^{2}Y(s) & =\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}+1}+s \\\\ {s}^{2}Y(s) & =\\frac{{s}^{3}+3s}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{{s}^{3}+3s}{{s}^{2}({s}^{2}+1)} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{{s}^{2}+3}{s({s}^{2}+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377893 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377894 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377893 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377895 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377893 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377896 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377893 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377897 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377893 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377898 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377893 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377893 1077 "selection" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377893 1079 #edb/ref 17592186377895 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377893 1080 #uuid "2c80bb07-d330-5d0e-9c92-22ba25529b38" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377894 1081 "Y(s)=\\frac{3}{s({s}^{2}+1)}" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377894 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377894 1084 #uuid "7bf80988-df4c-5b2c-acf7-eee7603bddbc" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377895 1081 "Y(s)=\\frac{{s}^{2}+3}{s({s}^{2}+1)}" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377895 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377895 1084 #uuid "c9ac8c1a-8262-533c-993e-d4e566b8b9fa" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377896 1081 "Y(s)=\\frac{2}{s({s}^{2}+1)}" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377896 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377896 1084 #uuid "ae51016c-b264-5a54-944a-0d1c12794ba1" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377897 1081 "Y(s)=\\frac{s+3}{s(s+1)}" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377897 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377897 1084 #uuid "3626aeb3-704c-53ca-a80b-a90f8153ce13" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377898 1081 "Y(s)=\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}+1}" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377898 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377898 1084 #uuid "5179aa8d-8b7a-5722-a053-7bc04e0068c0" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377899 1068 #edb/ref 17592186377900 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377899 1069 "q-83978" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377899 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377899 1071 "According to the Chinese remainder theorem, which of the following systems of congruences has a unique solution modulo $48?$ \n\n$$\n\\text{I.}\\,{\\begin{aligned}x≡4 & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\ x≡5 & (\\text{mod}\\,8)\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{II.}\\,{\\begin{aligned}x≡2 & (\\text{mod}\\,3) \\\\ x≡14 & (\\text{mod}\\,16)\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{III.}\\,{\\begin{aligned}x≡3 & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\ x≡7 & (\\text{mod}\\,12)\\end{aligned}\\,\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377899 1072 #uuid "0b00b20e-2b69-5a9e-a153-226cf102bd50" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377899 1074 "The Chinese remainder theorem states that if the natural numbers ${n}_{1},{n}_{2},…,{n}_{k}$ are pairwise coprime, meaning that $\\text{gcd}({n}_{i},{n}_{j})=1$ whenever $i\\ne j,$ then for any integers ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},…,{a}_{k}$ the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x≡{a}_{1} & (\\text{mod}\\,{n}_{1}) \\\\ x≡{a}_{2} & (\\text{mod}\\,{n}_{2}) \\\\ \\,⋮ \\\\ x≡{a}_{k} & (\\text{mod}\\,{n}_{k})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nhas a unique solution modulo ${n}_{1}{n}_{2}⋯{n}_{k}.$\n\nLet's now inspect each of the given systems in turn.\n\n- The theorem does *not* apply to system I since $6$ and $8$ are not coprime: \n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(6,8)=2>1\n$$\n\n- The theorem applies to system II. The numbers $3,$ and $16$ are pairwise coprime natural numbers. Therefore, according to the Chinese remainder theorem, the system has a unique solution modulo $3\\cdot 16=48.$\n\n- The theorem does *not* apply to system III since $4$ and $12$ are not coprime: \n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(4,12)=4>1\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377900 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377901 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377900 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377902 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377900 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377903 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186377900 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377904 13194139533452 17592186