 of catfish in the pond are given by the functions $45+5t$ and $1+t,$ respectively.\n\nGiven that the rate at which catfish are sold is $30$ per week, identify the expression that gives the number $C(t)$ of catfish in the pond at any time $t>0.$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378003 1072 #uuid "7766b214-876d-5b7f-81b6-3c5f1c9aaf26" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378003 1074 "Let $c(t)$ be the rate at which the number of catfish in the pond is changing. Then according to the given information,\n\n$$\nc(t)={{45+5t}_{⏟}}_{\\text{population increase}}-{{(1+t+30)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{population decrease}}=4t+14.\n$$\n\nLet $C(T)$ be the population of the pond $T$ weeks after the man started the business.  Using our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nC(T)=C(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}c(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(T) & =C(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}c(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =100+{\\int }_{0}^{T}(4t+14)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =100+(2{t}^{2}+14t){|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =100+2{T}^{2}+14T.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable $t.$ We have\n\n$$\nC(t)=100+2{t}^{2}+14t.\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378004 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378005 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378004 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378006 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378004 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378007 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378004 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378008 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378004 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378009 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378004 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378004 1077 "selection" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378004 1079 #edb/ref 17592186378008 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378004 1080 #uuid "702366f9-bbda-5814-95a0-2826f80f97b5" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378005 1081 "C(t)=94+4{t}^{2}+28t" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378005 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378005 1084 #uuid "38ca5715-6b66-553c-b648-d3b86d5364ab" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378006 1081 "C(t)=100+{t}^{2}+18t" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378006 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378006 1084 #uuid "592db95f-7e28-519f-b67a-4c9706986b93" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378007 1081 "C(t)=10+2{t}^{2}-14t" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378007 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378007 1084 #uuid "78da8694-4239-5b2e-a030-68cdcdb52280" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378008 1081 "C(t)=100+2{t}^{2}+14t" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378008 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378008 1084 #uuid "cde4b73a-c16f-52d3-ac81-f68655bfaee1" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378009 1081 "C(t)=86+2{t}^{2}+14t" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378009 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378009 1084 #uuid "e8ae0c59-e6d8-5ae8-857a-0be259247069" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378010 1068 #edb/ref 17592186378011 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378010 1069 "q-93883" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378010 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378010 1071 "Consider the Diophantine equation $7x+9y=11.$ The integer pair $(\\overset{∼}{x},\\overset{∼}{y})$ is the solution with the smallest possible positive value of $x.$ What is the value of $\\overset{∼}{x}?$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378010 1072 #uuid "07071c90-36c9-5e91-95a8-b12580d541fd" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378010 1074 "We have the linear Diophantine equation\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nwhere $a=7,$ $b=9,$ and $c=11.$ We're given that the equation has solutions.\n\nNotice that our equation can't be solved for either of the two variables using integer coefficients. So, let's find the linear representation of $\\text{gcd}(7,9)$ using the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}9 & = & 7\\cdot 1 & + & 2 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 7 & = & 2\\cdot 3 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the remainders (the rightmost terms) in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =9-7 \\\\ 1 & =7-2\\cdot 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =7-2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =7-3(9-7) \\\\  & =7\\cdot 4+(-3)\\cdot 9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that $d=\\text{gcd}(a,b)=1$ and\n\n$$\n7\\cdot 4+9\\cdot (-3)=1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying this equation by $11,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\cdot 4+9\\cdot (-3) & =1 \\\\ 7\\cdot 4\\cdot 11+9\\cdot (-3)\\cdot 11 & =11 \\\\ 7\\cdot 44+9\\cdot (-33) & =11,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that $(x,y)=(44,-33)$ is a solution to our Diophantine equation.\n\nNow, the general solution is\n\n$$\n(44+\\frac{b}{d}t,\\,-33-\\frac{a}{d}t)=(44+9t,\\,-33-7t),\n$$\n\nwhere $t=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…\\,.$\n\nThe smallest possible positive value of $x=44+9t$ is attained when $t=-4.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{∼}{x}=44+9\\cdot (-4)=8.\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378011 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378012 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378011 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378013 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378011 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378014 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378011 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378015 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378011 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378016 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378011 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378011 1077 "selection" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378011 1079 #edb/ref 17592186378015 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378011 1080 #uuid "69272b0c-6389-529c-91cc-0b8acb357691" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378012 1081 "10" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378012 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378012 1084 #uuid "df28ee37-b0cd-52f7-89fb-6d93ba4736fb" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378013 1081 "16" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378013 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378013 1084 #uuid "cbcbd1a1-f4cd-518b-ad26-e145640bdd7d" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378014 1081 "15" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378014 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378014 1084 #uuid "7f001cb1-7cd9-5373-a698-593e5c9563e1" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378015 1081 "8" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378015 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378015 1084 #uuid "1d4b3568-fa6d-5ece-acc7-492fea7b40cd" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378016 1081 "7" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378016 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378016 1084 #uuid "2f7e2df5-dfa1-5315-82e4-e4cda98fa09f" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378017 1068 #edb/ref 17592186378018 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378017 1069 "q-93884" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378017 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378017 1071 "Consider the Diophantine equation $7x+3y=5.$ The integer pair $(\\overset{∼}{x},\\overset{∼}{y})$ is the solution with the smallest possible positive value of $y.$ What is the value of $\\overset{∼}{y}?$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378017 1072 #uuid "e6daeb0c-4af1-5a7b-8f44-73ef5ee456eb" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378017 1074 "We have the linear Diophantine equation\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nwhere $a=7,$ $b=3,$ and $c=5.$ We're given that the equation has solutions.\n\nNotice that our equation can't be solved for either of the two variables using integer coefficients. So, let's find the linear representation of $\\text{gcd}(7,3)$ using the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}7 & = & 3\\cdot 2 & + & 1 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 3 & = & 1\\cdot 3 & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the remainders (the rightmost terms) in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =7-3\\cdot 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that $d=\\text{gcd}(a,b)=1$ and\n\n$$\n7+3\\cdot (-2)=1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying this equation by $5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7+3\\cdot (-2) & =1 \\\\ 7\\cdot 5+3\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 5 & =5 \\\\ 7\\cdot 5+3\\cdot (-10) & =5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that $(x,y)=(5,-10)$ is a solution to our Diophantine equation.\n\nNow, the general solution is\n\n$$\n(5+\\frac{b}{d}t,\\,-10-\\frac{a}{d}t)=(5+3t,\\,-10-7t),\n$$\n\nwhere $t=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…\\,.$\n\nThe smallest possible positive value of $y=-10-7t$ is attained when $t=-2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{∼}{y}=-10-7\\cdot (-2)=4.\n$$" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378018 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378019 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378018 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378020 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378018 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378021 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378018 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378022 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378018 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378023 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378018 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378018 1077 "selection" 13194139533452 17592186045585 true] [17592186378018 1079 #edb/ref 17592186378023 131